{"parentUuid":"13b16794-0609-4b4a-acb1-d4b5b8c89a52","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMWquker7HKLgU6y3ziJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NZmH7N4oV7QoGqDgdMGseK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\ndef edit(p, a, b):\n    s=open(p).read(); assert s.count(a)==1, (p, a[:50]); open(p,'w').write(s.replace(a,b))\nedit('/Users/suhaan/devenv/.claude/semantic-layer-plan.md',\n '#1 export fan-out + fail fast, M1 read paths off the exclusive lock, FTS rebuild + cache_size, #2, #11,',\n '#1 export fan-out + fail fast; **writers build-then-swap** (FTS rebuild, #11 pattern corpus, history writes: build outside `withBrainWriteLock`, take it only for the atomic swap). That is the M1 fix. The reader lock stays exactly as it is, because it linearizes responses against privacy tombstones. Then #2,')\nedit('/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/brain-timing/BRAIN-SPEEDUPS.md',\n 'and adds M1: read paths take the exclusive write lock, so brief fails with brain_locked after 10.5 s while any writer holds it. M1 is the new #2 priority.',\n 'and adds M1: brief fails with brain_locked after 10.5 s while any writer holds the lock. **Re-diagnosed by the Brain session:** the reader takes the lock only at response-write time, for microseconds. It is the erasure linearization boundary (`retrieval_transport.go:113`) and must stay. The cause is writers holding the same lock across full rebuilds (`history_fts.go:142/337`, `doc_fts.go:49`, `pattern_workspace.go:72`, `history.go:307`, `history_delta.go:1015`). Fix: build-then-swap on the writers. M1, #11 and the FTS rebuild are one fix.')\np='/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/process-map/process-map.html'\nedit(p,'[\"Retrieval stops taking the exclusive write lock: read without it, then re-check the privacy policy before answering\", \"brief and brain_brief stop failing during writes; first MCP brief 3.9 s → about 0.5 s\", \"meas\", \"new; failure reproduced at 10.5 s. A shared lock would not help, since it still waits for the writer\"],\\n  [\"Stop rebuilding the whole history search index after every change; build it where history changed, with a larger sqlite cache\", \"first query after each new session: about −5 s and −600 MB\", \"est\", \"new; the build is page-cache bound\"],',\n '[\"Writers build the new search index and pattern corpus outside the lock, then take it only to swap the file in\", \"brief and brain_brief stop failing during writes; first MCP brief 3.9 s → about 0.5 s; about −5 s and −600 MB on the first query after each new session\", \"meas\", \"new; failure reproduced at 10.5 s. Readers hold the lock for microseconds and must keep it, since it keeps responses consistent with privacy erasure. The fault is writers holding it for 8–32 s\"],')\nedit(p,'''<div class=\"callout\"><b>Agents' brief calls fail while Brain writes.</b> Read paths take the exclusive write lock, so <span class=\"mono\">brief</span> and MCP <span class=\"mono\">brain_brief</span> give up with <span class=\"mono\">brain_locked</span> after 10.5 s whenever a writer holds it. Writers hold it for 8–32 s, during setup, refresh after <span class=\"mono\">remember</span>, and background memory passes. Found by the review.</div>''',\n '''<div class=\"callout\"><b>Agents' brief calls fail while Brain writes.</b> <span class=\"mono\">brief</span> and MCP <span class=\"mono\">brain_brief</span> give up with <span class=\"mono\">brain_locked</span> after 10.5 s whenever a writer holds the Brain lock. Writers hold it for 8–32 s while rebuilding search indexes and the pattern corpus, during setup, refresh after <span class=\"mono\">remember</span>, and background memory passes. The fix is on the writer side: build outside the lock, then swap.</div>''')\nprint('ok')\nEOF","description":"Apply the M1 re-diagnosis to plan, fix list and artifact"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_