{"parentUuid":"8faa7bf5-5ff2-4378-9c1b-5c07e635fcce","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d7a781a5-000e-41f9-8f8a-4016475d88ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E5WnP5K6CMnmu6mWpBv9gK","type":"tool_result","content":"## 2. `build-then-swap`: one invariant, two options\n\n`TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes` asserts a tombstone\n**cannot** linearize during a staged publish. Any out-of-lock build turns that\ninto \"the tombstone proceeds, the swap aborts and rebuilds\".\n\nCompare-and-swap at publish preserves the end guarantee (no tombstoned data is\never published — a retry, not a window) but retires the stated non-interleaving\ninvariant. Rewriting a privacy test so a performance change passes is the move\nthat looks green and removes a guarantee, so it is yours.\n\n- **\"CAS is acceptable\"** → I implement it with a weakening proof.\n- **\"Non-interleaving stands\"** → closed; the lock-hold needs a different lever.\n\nFull note: `.claude/pending/build-then-swap-decision.md`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Transcript mode — costs money\n\n`export.go:2007` picks transcript mode **all-or-nothing**, so one session without\na compact transcript degrades every session to the raw form. Per-session mode\nre-keys the distill cache fingerprint, forcing a **paid corpus-wide re-distill**.\n\nNo longer urgent: #483 means this costs quality, not the whole history index.\nNote: `.claude/pending/linear-transcript-mode-global.md`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Destructive / outward, needs your word\n\n- **`brain-store-delete`** — 37 GB, irreversible. Sequence is written; I will not run it unasked.\n- **`B1-docs`** — 63 `~/Documents/Coding` paths on the cleanup list, three of them live.\n- **`linear-mcp-down`** — the connector needs removing and re-adding in claude.ai → Connectors. Three tickets are written and waiting; I cannot repair an account-side connector.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Product-shape calls (not defects)\n\n- **UX-23** — 41 verbs, 7 ways to ask a question.\n- **UX-29** — misleading command names.\n- **`privacy include`** — an escape hatch for a deliberately atomic privacy transaction. Blocked behind §3; the tombstone-first ordering I once called a bug is correct.\n- **cli-lane issues** (2) and **old PR drafts** (2 + 1 likely superseded) — written up, not ours to merge.\n\n---\n\n## Not waiting on you\n\n- **B2** (brain consumes `graph context --allow-ancestor`) — waiting on the graph session's G1 binary and JSON shape. Five consumer requirements adopted verbatim as a contract; building against a guessed shape is what that contract prevents.\n- **T-14** — answerable only by a multi-day `/brainusage` before/after.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Shrinking republish on a partial clone — the one real design call from the findings round (2026-10-25)\n\nFound by my own skeptic pass on **#489**, not by the review. #489 is still a clear\nimprovement on what it replaced, so this does not hold it — but it needs your call.\n\n**The measurement.** `#489` decides \"this object is gone\" from `git cat-file -e`'s exit\ncode, which I reproduced on real git 2.54.0: present = 0, resolved-but-absent = 1 silent,\nunresolvable = 128. Sound. But the probe runs under `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`, and on a\n**promisor partial clone** a blob that is merely *not fetched yet* also exits **1 silent** —\nbyte-identical to one destroyed forever. With lazy fetch allowed the same probe exits 0,\nbecause it fetches.\n\nSo under no-egress on a partial clone, \"absent\" is genuinely ambiguous and the code\n**cannot** tell the two apart without egress. #489 resolves it toward \"gone\" and skips the\nsession; `unavailableSessionsAreFatal` refuses only when *nothing* is servable. There is no\npartial-shrink guard, so a previously larger published corpus can be replaced by a smaller\none with only a warning.\n\n**This configuration is live.** `cli`'s brain was frozen for ten days precisely because\ntranscript and metadata blobs were promisor-absent; I unfroze it by fetching 422 of them by\nhand. Same promisor shape as graph #372.\n\n**Why I did not just fix it.** Both obvious fixes are wrong, and I checked both:\n- *\"a promisor remote exists, so absent might be fetchable → refuse\"* re-breaks #489 exactly\n  where it was needed: `cli` IS a promisor clone, and its squash-destroyed commits are\n  unfetchable anywhere.\n- *\"refuse if the new corpus is smaller\"* breaks `privacy exclude`, which legitimately\n  shrinks via tombstones — that is what **#488** does. It would also break #489's own\n  purpose, since genuinely destroyed sessions legitimately shrink the corpus too.\n\n**The actual question, which is a CLI-contract call and therefore yours:** should an export\nthat would drop previously-published sessions require explicit opt-in (a flag / a prompt),\nor keep publishing with a warning? That changes the command's contract, which is why I am\nnot deciding it. Recorded in brain at `architecture.export.constraint`.\n\n## 8. `internal/cli`'s prepush gate cannot pass under load (not a decision, a heads-up)\n\nMeasured independently by two agents and me: `internal/cli` runs **232s idle** and\n**805–855s under contention**, against `go test`'s **10m default**, which `test:prepush`\nnever raises (CI uses `-timeout 20m` in nightly/release). Under parallel load the gate fails\non correct code, and the panic names whichever test happened to be running at the alarm — it\nreads exactly like a flake in the file you just touched. Workaround is\n`GOFLAGS=-timeout=30m`; the real fix is a `-timeout` in the task, which I have not changed\nbecause it is your gate script.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3c6e9346-2275-40c0-a989-e6ae86c347c4","timestamp":"2026-10-25T05:45:34.892Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## 2. `build-then-swap`: one invariant, two options\n\n`TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes` asserts a tombstone\n**cannot** linearize during a staged publish. Any out-of-lock build turns that\ninto \"the tombstone proceeds, the swap aborts and rebuilds\".\n\nCompare-and-swap at publish preserves the end guarantee (no tombstoned data is\never published — a retry, not a window) but retires the stated non-interleaving\ninvariant. Rewriting a privacy test so a performance change passes is the move\nthat looks green and removes a guarantee, so it is yours.\n\n- **\"CAS is acceptable\"** → I implement it with a weakening proof.\n- **\"Non-interleaving stands\"** → closed; the lock-hold needs a different lever.\n\nFull note: `.claude/pending/build-then-swap-decision.md`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Transcript mode — costs money\n\n`export.go:2007` picks transcript mode **all-or-nothing**, so one session without\na compact transcript degrades every session to the raw form. Per-session mode\nre-keys the distill cache fingerprint, forcing a **paid corpus-wide re-distill**.\n\nNo longer urgent: #483 means this costs quality, not the whole history index.\nNote: `.claude/pending/linear-transcript-mode-global.md`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Destructive / outward, needs your word\n\n- **`brain-store-delete`** — 37 GB, irreversible. Sequence is written; I will not run it unasked.\n- **`B1-docs`** — 63 `~/Documents/Coding` paths on the cleanup list, three of them live.\n- **`linear-mcp-down`** — the connector needs removing and re-adding in claude.ai → Connectors. Three tickets are written and waiting; I cannot repair an account-side connector.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Product-shape calls (not defects)\n\n- **UX-23** — 41 verbs, 7 ways to ask a question.\n- **UX-29** — misleading command names.\n- **`privacy include`** — an escape hatch for a deliberately atomic privacy transaction. Blocked behind §3; the tombstone-first ordering I once called a bug is correct.\n- **cli-lane issues** (2) and **old PR drafts** (2 + 1 likely superseded) — written up, not ours to merge.\n\n---\n\n## Not waiting on you\n\n- **B2** (brain consumes `graph context --allow-ancestor`) — waiting on the graph session's G1 binary and JSON shape. Five consumer requirements adopted verbatim as a contract; building against a guessed shape is what that contract prevents.\n- **T-14** — answerable only by a multi-day `/brainusage` before/after.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Shrinking republish on a partial clone — the one real design call from the findings round (2026-10-25)\n\nFound by my own skeptic pass on **#489**, not by the review. #489 is still a clear\nimprovement on what it replaced, so this does not hold it — but it needs your call.\n\n**The measurement.** `#489` decides \"this object is gone\" from `git cat-file -e`'s exit\ncode, which I reproduced on real git 2.54.0: present = 0, resolved-but-absent = 1 silent,\nunresolvable = 128. Sound. But the probe runs under `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`, and on a\n**promisor partial clone** a blob that is merely *not fetched yet* also exits **1 silent** —\nbyte-identical to one destroyed forever. With lazy fetch allowed the same probe exits 0,\nbecause it fetches.\n\nSo under no-egress on a partial clone, \"absent\" is genuinely ambiguous and the code\n**cannot** tell the two apart without egress. #489 resolves it toward \"gone\" and skips the\nsession; `unavailableSessionsAreFatal` refuses only when *nothing* is servable. There is no\npartial-shrink guard, so a previously larger published corpus can be replaced by a smaller\none with only a warning.\n\n**This configuration is live.** `cli`'s brain was frozen for ten days precisely because\ntranscript and metadata blobs were promisor-absent; I unfroze it by fetching 422 of them by\nhand. Same promisor shape as graph #372.\n\n**Why I did not just fix it.** Both obvious fixes are wrong, and I checked both:\n- *\"a promisor remote exists, so absent might be fetchable → refuse\"* re-breaks #489 exactly\n  where it was needed: `cli` IS a promisor clone, and its squash-destroyed commits are\n  unfetchable anywhere.\n- *\"refuse if the new corpus is smaller\"* breaks `privacy exclude`, which legitimately\n  shrinks via tombstones — that is what **#488** does. It would also break #489's own\n  purpose, since genuinely destroyed sessions legitimately shrink the corpus too.\n\n**The actual question, which is a CLI-contract call and therefore yours:** should an export\nthat would drop previously-published sessions require explicit opt-in (a flag / a prompt),\nor keep publishing with a warning? That changes the command's contract, which is why I am\nnot deciding it. Recorded in brain at `architecture.export.constraint`.\n\n## 8. `internal/cli`'s prepush gate cannot pass under load (not a decision, a heads-up)\n\nMeasured independently by two agents and me: `internal/cli` runs **232s idle** and\n**805–855s under contention**, against `go test`'s **10m default**, which `test:prepush`\nnever raises (CI uses `-timeout 20m` in nightly/release). Under parallel load the gate fails\non correct code, and the panic names whichever test happened to be running at the alarm — it\nreads exactly like a flake in the file you just touched. Workaround is\n`GOFLAGS=-timeout=30m`; the real fix is a `-timeout` in the task, which I have not changed\nbecause it is your gate script.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"0f33b66e-c089-449d-abc2-ed51056f45b8","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":1,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":254},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/cli","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.