{"parentUuid":"ce52abc4-88a1-45a4-8110-16fa47d1be71","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/DECISIONS-FOR-YOU.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/DECISIONS-FOR-YOU.md","content":"# Decisions waiting on you — 2026-10-24\n\nEverything here needs your authority, not more engineering. Ordered by what\nunblocks the most.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Approve ten brain PRs (unblocks three other rows)\n\nAll ten: 25 CI checks green, *\"Agent review found no blocking issues\"*,\n**Approvals the only failing sub-gate**.\n\n| PR | what it fixes |\n|---|---|\n| #473 | watch.log lines carry no clock/repo attribution |\n| #474 | one divergent cached transcript aborted **every** export — froze a brain 13 days |\n| #476 | history terms re-normalised per candidate (9.8× slower, 23× more memory) |\n| #477 | MCP tool surface declared but never proven reachable |\n| #478 | one global fact fingerprint invalidated every branch's readers |\n| #479 | routed checkpoint details read one subprocess at a time (~1.75×) |\n| #480 | **routed list silently capped at 100 and described as \"complete\"** — could overwrite a full brain |\n| #481 | 97.7% of export subprocesses removed (5,721 → 134 on `cli`) |\n| #482 | six of eight agents hit a first-run refusal caused by the file Entire just wrote them |\n| #483 | one oversized transcript refused all 323 sessions |\n\n**Merge order: #479 before #481.** The rest are independent.\n\nApproving these also unblocks:\n- `refresh-heavy-deferred` — `cli`'s re-index costs 2.4h on today's binary, ~70s with #479+#481.\n- `oversized-transcript` — already downgraded from outage to cost decision by #483.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `build-then-swap`: one invariant, two options\n\n`TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes` asserts a tombstone\n**cannot** linearize during a staged publish. Any out-of-lock build turns that\ninto \"the tombstone proceeds, the swap aborts and rebuilds\".\n\nCompare-and-swap at publish preserves the end guarantee (no tombstoned data is\never published — a retry, not a window) but retires the stated non-interleaving\ninvariant. Rewriting a privacy test so a performance change passes is the move\nthat looks green and removes a guarantee, so it is yours.\n\n- **\"CAS is acceptable\"** → I implement it with a weakening proof.\n- **\"Non-interleaving stands\"** → closed; the lock-hold needs a different lever.\n\nFull note: `.claude/pending/build-then-swap-decision.md`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Transcript mode — costs money\n\n`export.go:2007` picks transcript mode **all-or-nothing**, so one session without\na compact transcript degrades every session to the raw form. Per-session mode\nre-keys the distill cache fingerprint, forcing a **paid corpus-wide re-distill**.\n\nNo longer urgent: #483 means this costs quality, not the whole history index.\nNote: `.claude/pending/linear-transcript-mode-global.md`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Destructive / outward, needs your word\n\n- **`brain-store-delete`** — 37 GB, irreversible. Sequence is written; I will not run it unasked.\n- **`B1-docs`** — 63 `~/Documents/Coding` paths on the cleanup list, three of them live.\n- **`linear-mcp-down`** — the connector needs removing and re-adding in claude.ai → Connectors. Three tickets are written and waiting; I cannot repair an account-side connector.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Product-shape calls (not defects)\n\n- **UX-23** — 41 verbs, 7 ways to ask a question.\n- **UX-29** — misleading command names.\n- **`privacy include`** — an escape hatch for a deliberately atomic privacy transaction. Blocked behind §3; the tombstone-first ordering I once called a bug is correct.\n- **cli-lane issues** (2) and **old PR drafts** (2 + 1 likely superseded) — written up, not ours to merge.\n\n---\n\n## Not waiting on you\n\n- **B2** (brain consumes `graph context --allow-ancestor`) — waiting on the graph session's G1 binary and JSON shape. Five consumer requirements adopted verbatim as a contract; building against a guessed shape is what that contract prevents.\n- **T-14** — answerable only by a multi-day `/brainusage` before/after.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Found by running the refresh you asked for (2026-10-24, late)\n\n**Brains refreshed.** `entire.io` jumped **2026-07-15 → 2026-10-25** (9,248\ncheckpoints, 3,384 sessions); `entire-brain`, `entire-graph`, `entiredb` are\ncurrent. Its seed phase then refused on 27 uncommitted paths that are your own\nwork — not committed, not stashed, not indexed.\n\n**`cli`: the blocker was SOLVED, and a smaller one is now yours.** Root cause\nwas a `blob:none` partial clone — ref, commit and tree all present while the\n**blobs were never fetched**, so `git ls-tree` made each checkpoint look\ncomplete while `explain --json` said \"checkpoint not found\" and **exited 0**.\nFetching one top-level `metadata.json` plus each `0/metadata.json` (422 blobs,\n~100 KB, 77s in a single batched promisor fetch) cleared the\n`checkpoint_scope_incomplete` refusal and took the discovered sessions from\n**2,375 to 2,561 (+186)**.\n\nIt now stops at the *write* phase on **one** session: `test` at checkpoint\n`18b787bf552f`. That checkpoint is not a valid git object in `cli`, no ref\nmentions it, the current brain has no session by that name, and it is absent\nfrom my earlier `--search-all` capture — so there is nothing local to fetch it\nby. I am **not** calling it pollution: 252 of 499 routed entries legitimately\nuse commit-style non-ULID ids, so the id shape proves nothing. Resolving it\nmeans either a fetch path I don't have or a cleanup on the **shared**\n`entireio/cli-checkpoints` remote, and I won't delete from a shared checkpoint\nremote. **Your call.**\n\n*(Superseded diagnosis, kept so the reasoning is auditable:)*\n**`cli`'s brain cannot refresh, and the diagnosis is new.** #481's shortcut\nworked in production — *\"resolved metadata for 5587 of 5768 checkpoints from the\nlocal checkpoint store; asked Entire about the remaining 181\"*, **3m56s instead\nof ~2.4h**. The export then correctly refused an incomplete catalog, leaving the\nbrain intact at 2026-10-15 (2,375 sessions; a clean pass would reach 2,514).\n\nThe 181 are a **cli bug, with evidence**: four sampled ids each have a live local\nref, a present commit object whose tree holds `0/metadata.json`, `0/full.jsonl`\nand `0/transcript.jsonl`, and no partial-clone involvement — yet\n`entire checkpoint explain --json <id>` prints `failed to read checkpoint:\ncheckpoint not found` **and exits 0**. The zero exit is why brain reads it as\nunreadable rather than as an error.\n\nThat earlier \"unresolved lead\" is now resolved and is **not** a defect: the\nsnapshot's 5,587 = **231 real refs + 5,356 read from the `entire/checkpoints/v1`\nbranch**. 231 is exactly the packed-ref count (231 packed + 147 loose = 378),\nand the 147 it lacks are exactly the loose refs, which postdate that probe — the\nref count grew 231 → 378 during the session as my own work created checkpoints.\nThe code's own enumeration returns the same 378 I get by hand, so it is not\nunder-enumerating. The union is refs + v1, as designed.\n\nSo the remaining blocker is the single cli bug above: an ID lookup that fails on\ndata which is present and well-formed, while exiting 0.\n\n**Graph indexing on `cli` and `entiredb` is blocked on graph #372.** Both refuse\nwith *\"its Git config declares a promisor remote (a partial clone)\"*. The\npromisor is on the **checkpoint remote, not `origin`** —\n`remote.https://github.com/entireio/cli-checkpoints.git.promisor=true`,\n`partialclonefilter=blob:none`. This machine runs git 2.54.0, above #372's\ncorrected 2.45.1 floor, so #372 admits them. It also explains why `entire-graph`\nis the only one of the five repos with a `semantic` source: it is the only one\nthat is not a partial clone. Relayed to the graph session.\n\n**Fixed, no decision needed:** devenv and `cli` had **no `SubagentStart` hook**,\nwhile entire-brain and entire-graph both wire one. Explore/Plan subagents never\nload CLAUDE.md, so every subagent spawned from here had never been told the\ngraph exists — the mechanical reason it looked like I wasn't using it. Both are\nwired now and `devenv/CLAUDE.md` records the hook plus its one-line check.\n\n## 7. ~~Shrinking republish on a partial clone~~ — CLOSED, no decision needed (2026-10-25)\n\n**I put this on your list and then took it off. It is fixed; nothing here needs you.**\nKept for the record because the reasoning is worth having.\n\n**The hole.** `#489` decides \"this object is gone\" from `git cat-file -e`'s exit code\n(reproduced on git 2.54.0: present 0, resolved-but-absent 1 silent, unresolvable 128). But\nthe probe runs under `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`, and in a **partial clone** a blob that is merely\n*unfetched* also exits 1 silent — identical to one destroyed forever. #489 skipped the\nsession, and `unavailableSessionsAreFatal` refuses only when *nothing* is servable, so a\nlarger published corpus could be replaced by a smaller one with just a warning. Live risk:\n`cli`'s brain was frozen for ten days on exactly this, and I unfroze it by fetching 422\nblobs by hand.\n\n**Why I first called it a product decision, and why that was wrong.** I believed the obvious\nfix — \"a promisor remote exists, so absent might be fetchable → refuse\" — would re-break\n#489 exactly where it was needed. It does not. The two cases take different paths:\n\n- a checkpoint whose **commit** was destroyed has no snapshot entry and no chunks, so it is\n  classified **from data** and never reaches git. That is #489's actual target case.\n- the git probe only ever sees a chunk the snapshot **does** name whose bytes are absent\n  locally — precisely the fetchable case.\n\nSo tightening the probe branch costs #489 nothing, and no CLI-contract change is required.\n\n**The fix.** In the confirmed-missing branch, if the source repository has a promisor remote\nthe absence is not definitive and the export **fails** instead of dropping the session. Full\nclones are unaffected: absent there is absent for good, and they still partition.\n\n**The detail worth keeping.** Detection is one local config read matched by **suffix** across\nevery remote (`config --get-regexp \\.promisor$`), because `cli`'s checkpoint repository names\nits promisor remote by URL — `remote.https://github.com/entireio/cli-checkpoints.git.promisor`\n— and does **not** set `extensions.partialclone`. A detector written against\n`remote.origin.promisor` or that extension passes every fake-runner test and misses the only\nrepository the guard exists for. Pinned against real git for that reason: narrowing the\npattern to `origin` turns the real-git test red while the fake-runner tests stay green.\n\n## 8. `internal/cli`'s prepush gate cannot pass under load (not a decision, a heads-up)\n\nMeasured independently by two agents and me: `internal/cli` runs **232s idle** and\n**805–855s under contention**, against `go test`'s **10m default**, which `test:prepush`\nnever raises (CI uses `-timeout 20m` in nightly/release). Under parallel load the gate fails\non correct code, and the panic names whichever test happened to be running at the alarm — it\nreads exactly like a flake in the file you just touched. Workaround is\n`GOFLAGS=-timeout=30m`; the real fix is a `-timeout` in the task, which I have not changed\nbecause it is your gate script.\n","numLines":199,"startLine":1,"totalLines":199}},"displayPath":"DECISIONS-FOR-YOU.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"023b1088-646f-49c3-b2af-9718eac31c97","timestamp":"2026-10-25T07:17:22.175Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv/DECISIONS-FOR-YOU.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Decisions waiting on you — 2026-10-24\n2\t\n3\tEverything here needs your authority, not more engineering. Ordered by what\n4\tunblocks the most.\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## 1. Approve ten brain PRs (unblocks three other rows)\n9\t\n10\tAll ten: 25 CI checks green, *\"Agent review found no blocking issues\"*,\n11\t**Approvals the only failing sub-gate**.\n12\t\n13\t| PR | what it fixes |\n14\t|---|---|\n15\t| #473 | watch.log lines carry no clock/repo attribution |\n16\t| #474 | one divergent cached transcript aborted **every** export — froze a brain 13 days |\n17\t| #476 | history terms re-normalised per candidate (9.8× slower, 23× more memory) |\n18\t| #477 | MCP tool surface declared but never proven reachable |\n19\t| #478 | one global fact fingerprint invalidated every branch's readers |\n20\t| #479 | routed checkpoint details read one subprocess at a time (~1.75×) |\n21\t| #480 | **routed list silently capped at 100 and described as \"complete\"** — could overwrite a full brain |\n22\t| #481 | 97.7% of export subprocesses removed (5,721 → 134 on `cli`) |\n23\t| #482 | six of eight agents hit a first-run refusal caused by the file Entire just wrote them |\n24\t| #483 | one oversized transcript refused all 323 sessions |\n25\t\n26\t**Merge order: #479 before #481.** The rest are independent.\n27\t\n28\tApproving these also unblocks:\n29\t- `refresh-heavy-deferred` — `cli`'s re-index costs 2.4h on today's binary, ~70s with #479+#481.\n30\t- `oversized-transcript` — already downgraded from outage to cost decision by #483.\n31\t\n32\t---\n33\t\n34\t## 2. `build-then-swap`: one invariant, two options\n35\t\n36\t`TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes` asserts a tombstone\n37\t**cannot** linearize during a staged publish. Any out-of-lock build turns that\n38\tinto \"the tombstone proceeds, the swap aborts and rebuilds\".\n39\t\n40\tCompare-and-swap at publish preserves the end guarantee (no tombstoned data is\n41\tever published — a retry, not a window) but retires the stated non-interleaving\n42\tinvariant. Rewriting a privacy test so a performance change passes is the move\n43\tthat looks green and removes a guarantee, so it is yours.\n44\t\n45\t- **\"CAS is acceptable\"** → I implement it with a weakening proof.\n46\t- **\"Non-interleaving stands\"** → closed; the lock-hold needs a different lever.\n47\t\n48\tFull note: `.claude/pending/build-then-swap-decision.md`\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 3. Transcript mode — costs money\n53\t\n54\t`export.go:2007` picks transcript mode **all-or-nothing**, so one session without\n55\ta compact transcript degrades every session to the raw form. Per-session mode\n56\tre-keys the distill cache fingerprint, forcing a **paid corpus-wide re-distill**.\n57\t\n58\tNo longer urgent: #483 means this costs quality, not the whole history index.\n59\tNote: `.claude/pending/linear-transcript-mode-global.md`\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## 4. Destructive / outward, needs your word\n64\t\n65\t- **`brain-store-delete`** — 37 GB, irreversible. Sequence is written; I will not run it unasked.\n66\t- **`B1-docs`** — 63 `~/Documents/Coding` paths on the cleanup list, three of them live.\n67\t- **`linear-mcp-down`** — the connector needs removing and re-adding in claude.ai → Connectors. Three tickets are written and waiting; I cannot repair an account-side connector.\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t## 5. Product-shape calls (not defects)\n72\t\n73\t- **UX-23** — 41 verbs, 7 ways to ask a question.\n74\t- **UX-29** — misleading command names.\n75\t- **`privacy include`** — an escape hatch for a deliberately atomic privacy transaction. Blocked behind §3; the tombstone-first ordering I once called a bug is correct.\n76\t- **cli-lane issues** (2) and **old PR drafts** (2 + 1 likely superseded) — written up, not ours to merge.\n77\t\n78\t---\n79\t\n80\t## Not waiting on you\n81\t\n82\t- **B2** (brain consumes `graph context --allow-ancestor`) — waiting on the graph session's G1 binary and JSON shape. Five consumer requirements adopted verbatim as a contract; building against a guessed shape is what that contract prevents.\n83\t- **T-14** — answerable only by a multi-day `/brainusage` before/after.\n84\t\n85\t---\n86\t\n87\t## 6. Found by running the refresh you asked for (2026-10-24, late)\n88\t\n89\t**Brains refreshed.** `entire.io` jumped **2026-07-15 → 2026-10-25** (9,248\n90\tcheckpoints, 3,384 sessions); `entire-brain`, `entire-graph`, `entiredb` are\n91\tcurrent. Its seed phase then refused on 27 uncommitted paths that are your own\n92\twork — not committed, not stashed, not indexed.\n93\t\n94\t**`cli`: the blocker was SOLVED, and a smaller one is now yours.** Root cause\n95\twas a `blob:none` partial clone — ref, commit and tree all present while the\n96\t**blobs were never fetched**, so `git ls-tree` made each checkpoint look\n97\tcomplete while `explain --json` said \"checkpoint not found\" and **exited 0**.\n98\tFetching one top-level `metadata.json` plus each `0/metadata.json` (422 blobs,\n99\t~100 KB, 77s in a single batched promisor fetch) cleared the\n100\t`checkpoint_scope_incomplete` refusal and took the discovered sessions from\n101\t**2,375 to 2,561 (+186)**.\n102\t\n103\tIt now stops at the *write* phase on **one** session: `test` at checkpoint\n104\t`18b787bf552f`. That checkpoint is not a valid git object in `cli`, no ref\n105\tmentions it, the current brain has no session by that name, and it is absent\n106\tfrom my earlier `--search-all` capture — so there is nothing local to fetch it\n107\tby. I am **not** calling it pollution: 252 of 499 routed entries legitimately\n108\tuse commit-style non-ULID ids, so the id shape proves nothing. Resolving it\n109\tmeans either a fetch path I don't have or a cleanup on the **shared**\n110\t`entireio/cli-checkpoints` remote, and I won't delete from a shared checkpoint\n111\tremote. **Your call.**\n112\t\n113\t*(Superseded diagnosis, kept so the reasoning is auditable:)*\n114\t**`cli`'s brain cannot refresh, and the diagnosis is new.** #481's shortcut\n115\tworked in production — *\"resolved metadata for 5587 of 5768 checkpoints from the\n116\tlocal checkpoint store; asked Entire about the remaining 181\"*, **3m56s instead\n117\tof ~2.4h**. The export then correctly refused an incomplete catalog, leaving the\n118\tbrain intact at 2026-10-15 (2,375 sessions; a clean pass would reach 2,514).\n119\t\n120\tThe 181 are a **cli bug, with evidence**: four sampled ids each have a live local\n121\tref, a present commit object whose tree holds `0/metadata.json`, `0/full.jsonl`\n122\tand `0/transcript.jsonl`, and no partial-clone involvement — yet\n123\t`entire checkpoint explain --json <id>` prints `failed to read checkpoint:\n124\tcheckpoint not found` **and exits 0**. The zero exit is why brain reads it as\n125\tunreadable rather than as an error.\n126\t\n127\tThat earlier \"unresolved lead\" is now resolved and is **not** a defect: the\n128\tsnapshot's 5,587 = **231 real refs + 5,356 read from the `entire/checkpoints/v1`\n129\tbranch**. 231 is exactly the packed-ref count (231 packed + 147 loose = 378),\n130\tand the 147 it lacks are exactly the loose refs, which postdate that probe — the\n131\tref count grew 231 → 378 during the session as my own work created checkpoints.\n132\tThe code's own enumeration returns the same 378 I get by hand, so it is not\n133\tunder-enumerating. The union is refs + v1, as designed.\n134\t\n135\tSo the remaining blocker is the single cli bug above: an ID lookup that fails on\n136\tdata which is present and well-formed, while exiting 0.\n137\t\n138\t**Graph indexing on `cli` and `entiredb` is blocked on graph #372.** Both refuse\n139\twith *\"its Git config declares a promisor remote (a partial clone)\"*. The\n140\tpromisor is on the **checkpoint remote, not `origin`** —\n141\t`remote.https://github.com/entireio/cli-checkpoints.git.promisor=true`,\n142\t`partialclonefilter=blob:none`. This machine runs git 2.54.0, above #372's\n143\tcorrected 2.45.1 floor, so #372 admits them. It also explains why `entire-graph`\n144\tis the only one of the five repos with a `semantic` source: it is the only one\n145\tthat is not a partial clone. Relayed to the graph session.\n146\t\n147\t**Fixed, no decision needed:** devenv and `cli` had **no `SubagentStart` hook**,\n148\twhile entire-brain and entire-graph both wire one. Explore/Plan subagents never\n149\tload CLAUDE.md, so every subagent spawned from here had never been told the\n150\tgraph exists — the mechanical reason it looked like I wasn't using it. Both are\n151\twired now and `devenv/CLAUDE.md` records the hook plus its one-line check.\n152\t\n153\t## 7. ~~Shrinking republish on a partial clone~~ — CLOSED, no decision needed (2026-10-25)\n154\t\n155\t**I put this on your list and then took it off. It is fixed; nothing here needs you.**\n156\tKept for the record because the reasoning is worth having.\n157\t\n158\t**The hole.** `#489` decides \"this object is gone\" from `git cat-file -e`'s exit code\n159\t(reproduced on git 2.54.0: present 0, resolved-but-absent 1 silent, unresolvable 128). But\n160\tthe probe runs under `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`, and in a **partial clone** a blob that is merely\n161\t*unfetched* also exits 1 silent — identical to one destroyed forever. #489 skipped the\n162\tsession, and `unavailableSessionsAreFatal` refuses only when *nothing* is servable, so a\n163\tlarger published corpus could be replaced by a smaller one with just a warning. Live risk:\n164\t`cli`'s brain was frozen for ten days on exactly this, and I unfroze it by fetching 422\n165\tblobs by hand.\n166\t\n167\t**Why I first called it a product decision, and why that was wrong.** I believed the obvious\n168\tfix — \"a promisor remote exists, so absent might be fetchable → refuse\" — would re-break\n169\t#489 exactly where it was needed. It does not. The two cases take different paths:\n170\t\n171\t- a checkpoint whose **commit** was destroyed has no snapshot entry and no chunks, so it is\n172\t  classified **from data** and never reaches git. That is #489's actual target case.\n173\t- the git probe only ever sees a chunk the snapshot **does** name whose bytes are absent\n174\t  locally — precisely the fetchable case.\n175\t\n176\tSo tightening the probe branch costs #489 nothing, and no CLI-contract change is required.\n177\t\n178\t**The fix.** In the confirmed-missing branch, if the source repository has a promisor remote\n179\tthe absence is not definitive and the export **fails** instead of dropping the session. Full\n180\tclones are unaffected: absent there is absent for good, and they still partition.\n181\t\n182\t**The detail worth keeping.** Detection is one local config read matched by **suffix** across\n183\tevery remote (`config --get-regexp \\.promisor$`), because `cli`'s checkpoint repository names\n184\tits promisor remote by URL — `remote.https://github.com/entireio/cli-checkpoints.git.promisor`\n185\t— and does **not** set `extensions.partialclone`. A detector written against\n186\t`remote.origin.promisor` or that extension passes every fake-runner test and misses the only\n187\trepository the guard exists for. Pinned against real git for that reason: narrowing the\n188\tpattern to `origin` turns the real-git test red while the fake-runner tests stay green.\n189\t\n190\t## 8. `internal/cli`'s prepush gate cannot pass under load (not a decision, a heads-up)\n191\t\n192\tMeasured independently by two agents and me: `internal/cli` runs **232s idle** and\n193\t**805–855s under contention**, against `go test`'s **10m default**, which `test:prepush`\n194\tnever raises (CI uses `-timeout 20m` in nightly/release). Under parallel load the gate fails\n195\ton correct code, and the panic names whichever test happened to be running at the alarm — it\n196\treads exactly like a flake in the file you just touched. Workaround is\n197\t`GOFLAGS=-timeout=30m`; the real fix is a `-timeout` in the task, which I have not changed\n198\tbecause it is your gate script.\n199\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
{"parentUuid":"023b1088-646f-49c3-b2af-9718eac31c97","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/HANDOFF-GRAPH.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/HANDOFF-GRAPH.md","content":"# Graph merge handoff (entireio/entire-graph)\n\nAll 12 PRs below are CI-green, with 0 open findings: checked per trail with\n`--trail entire-graph/<n> --status any --freshness any`, and trail.branch matched against the PR head. Each is\nblocked only on the Approvals sub-gate. Draft PR **#381** (DO NOT MERGE) combines all 12 in this order. CI on its\nhead b0a4ba48 is green, and the full local `internal/sem`, `internal/cli` and `internal/gitutil` suites pass, plus\n148 key tests under `-race`.\n\n## Merge order\nMerge with a merge commit. Retarget #377 to `main` once #371 has merged.\n\n| # | PR | What | Measured | Rebase needed? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | #370 | gitignore `.claude/worktrees/` | Brain seed no longer refuses on nested worktrees | no |\n| 2 | #371 | Alias git grep: corpus paths only, `-l` | entire-graph 224 s → 5.1 s; brain 52 → 9.6 s; cli 12 → 4.9 s | no |\n| 3 | #377 | Cold query uses warm preselection (stacked on #371) | cold == warm (before, 20-59 fields differed); first query −2.5 to −3.9 s; eg alias +0.8 s, disclosed | retarget to main |\n| 4 | #373 | Ordered pipeline window ×16 | cold index −28% to −35% on 4 repos, sha-identical | no |\n| 5 | #379 | Search-store retention (K=3, 1 GiB) | 6 commits → 2 stores; HEAD and just-written stores never evicted | no |\n| 6 | #376 | Stop persisting the derived selective view | warm query −0.16 to −0.38 s | no |\n| 7 | #375 | Cache-hit history splice | warm def/neighbors/impact 0.62 → 0.44 s (fixes the regression) | no |\n| 8 | #372 | Index partial clones on Git ≥ 2.45.1 (no network egress) | cli/entiredb index offline; `query --head` cli 34 → 2.5 s. Three adversarial rounds: SAFE TO MERGE | CHANGELOG only |\n| 9 | #380 | `graph context --allow-ancestor N` + capability key | serves HEAD~1 in 350 ms with a typed stale block | CHANGELOG only |\n| 10 | #374 | Tool-handler heuristic reads identifiers only | false tool symbols 84 → 3; cache bump v22/v27 | CHANGELOG only |\n| 11 | #142 | Stop cleanly on cancellation; never cache a truncated snapshot | behaviour fix (time limit dropped) | **yes**: apply `142-x-374.patch`, `142-x-376.patch`, `142-x-378-allowlist.patch` |\n| 12 | #378 | Disclose unmodelled value refs + co-change failure warning; cache bump v23/v28 | silent gaps now warn | **yes**: `375-x-378.patch`, `142-x-378.patch`, `374-x-378-versions.patch`, plus CHANGELOG |\n\nPatches: `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-integration/.g3/resolutions/`. They are also embedded in #381's body.\n\n## Combined effect (cli repo, #381 vs main)\n- cold index 11.8 → 7.8 s (−34%)\n- first warm query 11.7 → 3.5 s (−70%)\n- warm def/neighbors/impact 0.62 → 0.44 s\n\n## Your decisions\n- **Approvals** on all 12. You cannot approve your own PRs, so a teammate is needed, or your override.\n- Whether to make the new `lazy-fetch git matrix` CI job (#372) a required check. It is path-filtered.\n- Close #381 after the merges. It exists only for CI.\n- Your two old drafts: #146 (RFD 0005) and #300 (semantic channel). Community #249 is probably superseded by #261.\n\n## Follow-ups noted, not done\n- `--no-renames` in co-change, so it works on blob:none clones.\n- A failing history refresh makes every warm query a cache miss.\n- A faster warm alias route, which would remove #377's +0.8 s.\n- Real C1 fix: Go var symbols plus a REFERENCES relation.\n- Python and TypeScript tool-registration detection.\n- Static launch-scan gaps: reflect, plugin, asm, computed syscalls.\n","numLines":47,"startLine":1,"totalLines":47}},"displayPath":"../devenv-worktrees/timing/HANDOFF-GRAPH.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6b59d1ac-ec55-4eda-84eb-ca0ed935048d","timestamp":"2026-10-25T07:17:22.175Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/HANDOFF-GRAPH.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Graph merge handoff (entireio/entire-graph)\n2\t\n3\tAll 12 PRs below are CI-green, with 0 open findings: checked per trail with\n4\t`--trail entire-graph/<n> --status any --freshness any`, and trail.branch matched against the PR head. Each is\n5\tblocked only on the Approvals sub-gate. Draft PR **#381** (DO NOT MERGE) combines all 12 in this order. CI on its\n6\thead b0a4ba48 is green, and the full local `internal/sem`, `internal/cli` and `internal/gitutil` suites pass, plus\n7\t148 key tests under `-race`.\n8\t\n9\t## Merge order\n10\tMerge with a merge commit. Retarget #377 to `main` once #371 has merged.\n11\t\n12\t| # | PR | What | Measured | Rebase needed? |\n13\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n14\t| 1 | #370 | gitignore `.claude/worktrees/` | Brain seed no longer refuses on nested worktrees | no |\n15\t| 2 | #371 | Alias git grep: corpus paths only, `-l` | entire-graph 224 s → 5.1 s; brain 52 → 9.6 s; cli 12 → 4.9 s | no |\n16\t| 3 | #377 | Cold query uses warm preselection (stacked on #371) | cold == warm (before, 20-59 fields differed); first query −2.5 to −3.9 s; eg alias +0.8 s, disclosed | retarget to main |\n17\t| 4 | #373 | Ordered pipeline window ×16 | cold index −28% to −35% on 4 repos, sha-identical | no |\n18\t| 5 | #379 | Search-store retention (K=3, 1 GiB) | 6 commits → 2 stores; HEAD and just-written stores never evicted | no |\n19\t| 6 | #376 | Stop persisting the derived selective view | warm query −0.16 to −0.38 s | no |\n20\t| 7 | #375 | Cache-hit history splice | warm def/neighbors/impact 0.62 → 0.44 s (fixes the regression) | no |\n21\t| 8 | #372 | Index partial clones on Git ≥ 2.45.1 (no network egress) | cli/entiredb index offline; `query --head` cli 34 → 2.5 s. Three adversarial rounds: SAFE TO MERGE | CHANGELOG only |\n22\t| 9 | #380 | `graph context --allow-ancestor N` + capability key | serves HEAD~1 in 350 ms with a typed stale block | CHANGELOG only |\n23\t| 10 | #374 | Tool-handler heuristic reads identifiers only | false tool symbols 84 → 3; cache bump v22/v27 | CHANGELOG only |\n24\t| 11 | #142 | Stop cleanly on cancellation; never cache a truncated snapshot | behaviour fix (time limit dropped) | **yes**: apply `142-x-374.patch`, `142-x-376.patch`, `142-x-378-allowlist.patch` |\n25\t| 12 | #378 | Disclose unmodelled value refs + co-change failure warning; cache bump v23/v28 | silent gaps now warn | **yes**: `375-x-378.patch`, `142-x-378.patch`, `374-x-378-versions.patch`, plus CHANGELOG |\n26\t\n27\tPatches: `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-integration/.g3/resolutions/`. They are also embedded in #381's body.\n28\t\n29\t## Combined effect (cli repo, #381 vs main)\n30\t- cold index 11.8 → 7.8 s (−34%)\n31\t- first warm query 11.7 → 3.5 s (−70%)\n32\t- warm def/neighbors/impact 0.62 → 0.44 s\n33\t\n34\t## Your decisions\n35\t- **Approvals** on all 12. You cannot approve your own PRs, so a teammate is needed, or your override.\n36\t- Whether to make the new `lazy-fetch git matrix` CI job (#372) a required check. It is path-filtered.\n37\t- Close #381 after the merges. It exists only for CI.\n38\t- Your two old drafts: #146 (RFD 0005) and #300 (semantic channel). Community #249 is probably superseded by #261.\n39\t\n40\t## Follow-ups noted, not done\n41\t- `--no-renames` in co-change, so it works on blob:none clones.\n42\t- A failing history refresh makes every warm query a cache miss.\n43\t- A faster warm alias route, which would remove #377's +0.8 s.\n44\t- Real C1 fix: Go var symbols plus a REFERENCES relation.\n45\t- Python and TypeScript tool-registration detection.\n46\t- Static launch-scan gaps: reflect, plugin, asm, computed syscalls.\n47\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
{"parentUuid":"6b59d1ac-ec55-4eda-84eb-ca0ed935048d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md","content":"# G3 fix protocol (entire-graph speed and correctness fixes)\n\nEvery G3 fix agent follows this file. The item-specific brief comes in the agent's prompt.\n\n## Inputs\n- Fix list: `GRAPH-SPEEDUPS.md`. Review, which is authoritative where it disagrees: `GRAPH-SPEEDUPS-REVIEW.md`.\n- Step maps: `PROCESS-MAP.md`, `stepmaps/`. Reviewer tools: `g2-measure.sh`, `g2-strip.py`, `g2-patterns/`, `g2-v2/`, `raw/g2/`.\n- All of these live in `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/`.\n\n## Workspace\n- One fresh worktree per fix, off `origin/main`:\n  `git -C /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph fetch -q origin main && git -C /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph worktree add -b <branch> /Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-<short-name> origin/main`\n- `/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph` is a main checkout. Read from it; never edit, commit, or switch branches there.\n- **Scratch files go in your worktree** (for example `<worktree>/.g3/`, never committed), not the shared session scratchpad or `/tmp`. Parallel agents share the scratchpad and have overwritten each other's PR bodies.\n- No `git stash`: one stash is shared across all worktrees, and parallel agents collide.\n- Commit with the repo's configured identity. **Never** pass `-c user.email=...` or `--author`.\n- Conventional commit subject (`perf(sem): ...`, `fix(cli): ...`). **No AI or Claude attribution lines** in commits or PR bodies. No references to people or conversations.\n\n## Tools: graph and brain first\n1. Preflight once:\n   - `entire graph version --json`, `entire graph capabilities --json`\n   - `entire brain version`, `entire brain capabilities --json`\n2. Before editing any exported or shared symbol, run `entire graph impact --repo <worktree> --head --symbol <Name>` and cite the result. Use `def` and `neighbors --direction in` for callers. grep only supplements; it never replaces these.\n3. For why code exists or prior decisions, run `entire brain recall \"<question>\" --json` from `/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph`. An empty result is not proof.\n\n## CPU, measuring and coordination\n- Other agents run in parallel on this machine. **Any timing run, cold index, or test run longer than about a minute must hold the measure lock:**\n  `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/mlock '<command>'` (flock(1) is not installed; mlock takes the same fcntl lock)\n  Keep each locked block short (minutes, not an hour) so the others are not starved.\n- Before a timing block, check two things:\n  - `uptime`: record load1. Don't measure above about 8.\n  - `/Users/suhaan/devenv/.claude/todo-board.json`: any row with `\"lane\": \"brain\"`, `\"status\": \"doing\"` and a title starting `measuring:` means wait until it clears.\n- Focused tests: `env -u FORCE_COLOR GOMAXPROCS=4 go test ./<pkg> -run '<Regex>' -count=1`. The shell sets FORCE_COLOR=3, which breaks colour-assert tests on correct code.\n- **entire-graph CI does NOT run `-race`** (test.yml:118 runs plain `go test ./...`; brain's CI does). **Your local `-race` run is the only race evidence**, so it is mandatory. Run your focused tests with `-race` too, under mlock, before trusting a local green. Watch for anything that reads a subprocess's captured `Stdout`/`Stderr` (a `bytes.Buffer` or `strings.Builder`) before `cmd.Wait()`: os/exec copies into it from its own goroutine, so that is a data race.\n- If CI shows a broad cascade of unrelated failures, **grep the log for `DATA RACE` first.** One race poisons the whole run and looks exactly like resource exhaustion.\n- **No full local suites.** CI runs them. Exception: the package you changed may be run whole, under the lock, with `-p 1`.\n- Never `--daemon`, `entire brain setup` or `entire brain refresh`. Never touch `~/.local/share/entire`.\n\n## Before/after timing (speed fixes)\n- Build two binaries into your worktree's `bin/` (not `/tmp`, which other agents share): `bin/base` from `origin/main` and `bin/new` from your branch.\n- Interleave base/new runs. Use a separate `--cache-dir` per binary, and a fresh one per cold run. 3 reps, report the median. Use the same repos as the study (clones under `timing/repos/`, or `/Users/suhaan/devenv/{entire-graph,entire-brain}`).\n- **Output identity:** compare base vs new JSON outputs byte-for-byte after `g2-strip.py` (it strips timing fields), or the snapshot sha256. If the output is meant to change, say exactly how and test it.\n- The index cache is keyed by tree, not binary, so base and new must never share a cache dir.\n\n## Tests: mutation-proven\n- Add or adjust a test that pins the new behaviour.\n- **Verified by weakening:** temporarily break the guard or fix, show the test goes red (paste the failing line), then restore it and verify the restore by `git diff`.\n- A test that cannot go red is not a test.\n- **Commit before weakening, and restore each mutation from a byte copy (checked with `cmp`), never with `git checkout -- <file>`.** `git checkout` restores the last COMMIT, so it silently wipes any uncommitted edit in that file. That happened once on #372.\n- **Fake subprocesses that answer and exit must drain stdin** (`cat >/dev/null &` … `wait`). Otherwise the caller's write to stdin races the exit and fails with EPIPE (\"broken pipe\") on CI while passing locally. Fake `git` used with `grep -f -` is exactly this shape.\n- **When a guard depends on goroutine timing, make the fixture force the order.** For example, a fake that closes stdout first, sleeps, then writes its reason. Don't hope the race lands the same way on CI: on macOS one side may win every time, so the weakening passes locally and only CI disagrees.\n- Watch the Windows traps: filepath separators, CRLF checkouts, and platform branches that nothing executes.\n\n## PR\n- `git push -u origin <branch>`, then `gh pr create --repo entireio/entire-graph --base main`.\n- Body sections:\n  - **Summary:** what and why.\n  - **Measurements:** a base vs new table with medians, reps, load1, repos, and the identity check.\n  - **Test plan:** the tests, and what you weakened to prove them.\n- Do NOT merge, approve, request reviewers, or create an Entire trail by hand.\n- Watch CI with `gh pr checks <n> --repo entireio/entire-graph`. Fix real failures.\n  - A running check has conclusion `\"\"`. It has not failed.\n  - `test (macos-latest)` near 20 minutes is usually the suite timeout, not your diff. Check the log before changing code.\n- If an Entire Gates agent review posts findings on the trail, address the real ones: fix the code, or explain in a PR comment.\n  - The installed `~/.local/bin/entire` is a fresh build of cli main. Finding commands take the **org-level trail number** from the PR body link `trails/<ORG>/changes/entire-graph/<REPO>`, so use `<ORG>`, e.g. `entire trail finding list 2516 --json`. The per-repo number (the gate link's `trails/290`) selects a DIFFERENT trail in the new CLI.\n  - Unambiguous form: `entire trail finding list --trail entire-graph/<REPO#> --status any --freshness any --json`. Then **check `trail.branch` equals your PR's head branch** before trusting any count. A misaddressed trail answers \"no open changes\", which reads exactly like a clean review.\n  - Don't trust the gate's \"Findings: passed\": a review can post after the check completes. `entire trail finding list <ORG> --status any --freshness any --json` is the source of truth.\n  - Keep the Entire trail link block at the top of the PR body when you rewrite it.\n\n## Report (caveman-ultra, ≤ 40 lines)\nInclude:\n- the PR number and branch;\n- the base → new medians with load;\n- the identity check result;\n- the tests and the weakening proof;\n- CI state, enumerated as green, failed, or running;\n- anything you could not finish, and why.\n\nUse RTK for shell (auto-rewritten; `rtk proxy <cmd>` for raw).\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}},"displayPath":"../devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2b56d9c2-f9f5-4e43-b981-cbf50e23d583","timestamp":"2026-10-25T07:17:22.175Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/G3-PROTOCOL.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# G3 fix protocol (entire-graph speed and correctness fixes)\n2\t\n3\tEvery G3 fix agent follows this file. The item-specific brief comes in the agent's prompt.\n4\t\n5\t## Inputs\n6\t- Fix list: `GRAPH-SPEEDUPS.md`. Review, which is authoritative where it disagrees: `GRAPH-SPEEDUPS-REVIEW.md`.\n7\t- Step maps: `PROCESS-MAP.md`, `stepmaps/`. Reviewer tools: `g2-measure.sh`, `g2-strip.py`, `g2-patterns/`, `g2-v2/`, `raw/g2/`.\n8\t- All of these live in `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/`.\n9\t\n10\t## Workspace\n11\t- One fresh worktree per fix, off `origin/main`:\n12\t  `git -C /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph fetch -q origin main && git -C /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph worktree add -b <branch> /Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-<short-name> origin/main`\n13\t- `/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph` is a main checkout. Read from it; never edit, commit, or switch branches there.\n14\t- **Scratch files go in your worktree** (for example `<worktree>/.g3/`, never committed), not the shared session scratchpad or `/tmp`. Parallel agents share the scratchpad and have overwritten each other's PR bodies.\n15\t- No `git stash`: one stash is shared across all worktrees, and parallel agents collide.\n16\t- Commit with the repo's configured identity. **Never** pass `-c user.email=...` or `--author`.\n17\t- Conventional commit subject (`perf(sem): ...`, `fix(cli): ...`). **No AI or Claude attribution lines** in commits or PR bodies. No references to people or conversations.\n18\t\n19\t## Tools: graph and brain first\n20\t1. Preflight once:\n21\t   - `entire graph version --json`, `entire graph capabilities --json`\n22\t   - `entire brain version`, `entire brain capabilities --json`\n23\t2. Before editing any exported or shared symbol, run `entire graph impact --repo <worktree> --head --symbol <Name>` and cite the result. Use `def` and `neighbors --direction in` for callers. grep only supplements; it never replaces these.\n24\t3. For why code exists or prior decisions, run `entire brain recall \"<question>\" --json` from `/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph`. An empty result is not proof.\n25\t\n26\t## CPU, measuring and coordination\n27\t- Other agents run in parallel on this machine. **Any timing run, cold index, or test run longer than about a minute must hold the measure lock:**\n28\t  `/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/timing/mlock '<command>'` (flock(1) is not installed; mlock takes the same fcntl lock)\n29\t  Keep each locked block short (minutes, not an hour) so the others are not starved.\n30\t- Before a timing block, check two things:\n31\t  - `uptime`: record load1. Don't measure above about 8.\n32\t  - `/Users/suhaan/devenv/.claude/todo-board.json`: any row with `\"lane\": \"brain\"`, `\"status\": \"doing\"` and a title starting `measuring:` means wait until it clears.\n33\t- Focused tests: `env -u FORCE_COLOR GOMAXPROCS=4 go test ./<pkg> -run '<Regex>' -count=1`. The shell sets FORCE_COLOR=3, which breaks colour-assert tests on correct code.\n34\t- **entire-graph CI does NOT run `-race`** (test.yml:118 runs plain `go test ./...`; brain's CI does). **Your local `-race` run is the only race evidence**, so it is mandatory. Run your focused tests with `-race` too, under mlock, before trusting a local green. Watch for anything that reads a subprocess's captured `Stdout`/`Stderr` (a `bytes.Buffer` or `strings.Builder`) before `cmd.Wait()`: os/exec copies into it from its own goroutine, so that is a data race.\n35\t- If CI shows a broad cascade of unrelated failures, **grep the log for `DATA RACE` first.** One race poisons the whole run and looks exactly like resource exhaustion.\n36\t- **No full local suites.** CI runs them. Exception: the package you changed may be run whole, under the lock, with `-p 1`.\n37\t- Never `--daemon`, `entire brain setup` or `entire brain refresh`. Never touch `~/.local/share/entire`.\n38\t\n39\t## Before/after timing (speed fixes)\n40\t- Build two binaries into your worktree's `bin/` (not `/tmp`, which other agents share): `bin/base` from `origin/main` and `bin/new` from your branch.\n41\t- Interleave base/new runs. Use a separate `--cache-dir` per binary, and a fresh one per cold run. 3 reps, report the median. Use the same repos as the study (clones under `timing/repos/`, or `/Users/suhaan/devenv/{entire-graph,entire-brain}`).\n42\t- **Output identity:** compare base vs new JSON outputs byte-for-byte after `g2-strip.py` (it strips timing fields), or the snapshot sha256. If the output is meant to change, say exactly how and test it.\n43\t- The index cache is keyed by tree, not binary, so base and new must never share a cache dir.\n44\t\n45\t## Tests: mutation-proven\n46\t- Add or adjust a test that pins the new behaviour.\n47\t- **Verified by weakening:** temporarily break the guard or fix, show the test goes red (paste the failing line), then restore it and verify the restore by `git diff`.\n48\t- A test that cannot go red is not a test.\n49\t- **Commit before weakening, and restore each mutation from a byte copy (checked with `cmp`), never with `git checkout -- <file>`.** `git checkout` restores the last COMMIT, so it silently wipes any uncommitted edit in that file. That happened once on #372.\n50\t- **Fake subprocesses that answer and exit must drain stdin** (`cat >/dev/null &` … `wait`). Otherwise the caller's write to stdin races the exit and fails with EPIPE (\"broken pipe\") on CI while passing locally. Fake `git` used with `grep -f -` is exactly this shape.\n51\t- **When a guard depends on goroutine timing, make the fixture force the order.** For example, a fake that closes stdout first, sleeps, then writes its reason. Don't hope the race lands the same way on CI: on macOS one side may win every time, so the weakening passes locally and only CI disagrees.\n52\t- Watch the Windows traps: filepath separators, CRLF checkouts, and platform branches that nothing executes.\n53\t\n54\t## PR\n55\t- `git push -u origin <branch>`, then `gh pr create --repo entireio/entire-graph --base main`.\n56\t- Body sections:\n57\t  - **Summary:** what and why.\n58\t  - **Measurements:** a base vs new table with medians, reps, load1, repos, and the identity check.\n59\t  - **Test plan:** the tests, and what you weakened to prove them.\n60\t- Do NOT merge, approve, request reviewers, or create an Entire trail by hand.\n61\t- Watch CI with `gh pr checks <n> --repo entireio/entire-graph`. Fix real failures.\n62\t  - A running check has conclusion `\"\"`. It has not failed.\n63\t  - `test (macos-latest)` near 20 minutes is usually the suite timeout, not your diff. Check the log before changing code.\n64\t- If an Entire Gates agent review posts findings on the trail, address the real ones: fix the code, or explain in a PR comment.\n65\t  - The installed `~/.local/bin/entire` is a fresh build of cli main. Finding commands take the **org-level trail number** from the PR body link `trails/<ORG>/changes/entire-graph/<REPO>`, so use `<ORG>`, e.g. `entire trail finding list 2516 --json`. The per-repo number (the gate link's `trails/290`) selects a DIFFERENT trail in the new CLI.\n66\t  - Unambiguous form: `entire trail finding list --trail entire-graph/<REPO#> --status any --freshness any --json`. Then **check `trail.branch` equals your PR's head branch** before trusting any count. A misaddressed trail answers \"no open changes\", which reads exactly like a clean review.\n67\t  - Don't trust the gate's \"Findings: passed\": a review can post after the check completes. `entire trail finding list <ORG> --status any --freshness any --json` is the source of truth.\n68\t  - Keep the Entire trail link block at the top of the PR body when you rewrite it.\n69\t\n70\t## Report (caveman-ultra, ≤ 40 lines)\n71\tInclude:\n72\t- the PR number and branch;\n73\t- the base → new medians with load;\n74\t- the identity check result;\n75\t- the tests and the weakening proof;\n76\t- CI state, enumerated as green, failed, or running;\n77\t- anything you could not finish, and why.\n78\t\n79\tUse RTK for shell (auto-rewritten; `rtk proxy <cmd>` for raw).\n80\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus"}
{"parentUuid":"2b56d9c2-f9f5-4e43-b981-cbf50e23d583","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/brain-timing/BRAIN-SPEEDUPS.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/brain-timing/BRAIN-SPEEDUPS.md","content":"# Brain: proposed speed and efficiency fixes\n\n> **Read BRAIN-SPEEDUPS-REVIEW.md first.** It corrects several estimates (realistic cold setup is about 65-80 s, not 45-55 s), splits #6, refutes parts of #1/#7/#8, and adds M1: brief fails with brain_locked after 10.5 s while any writer holds the lock. **Re-diagnosed by the Brain session:** the reader takes the lock only at response-write time, for microseconds. It is the erasure linearization boundary (`retrieval_transport.go:113`) and must stay. The cause is writers holding the same lock across full rebuilds (`history_fts.go:142/337`, `doc_fts.go:49`, `pattern_workspace.go:72`, `history.go:307`, `history_delta.go:1015`). Fix: build-then-swap on the writers. M1, #11 and the FTS rebuild are one fix.\n\nMeasured 2026-10-24 on entire-brain `main` 318e4b05 (= installed v0.2.0-nightly), with the graph at d4a0c186.\nIsolated store, no daemon. Quiet-machine numbers (load 7-10).\nFull process maps: `PROCESS-MAP.md`. Raw data: `results/`. Profiles: `profiles/`, `results/pprof/`.\n\n**Status: proposals only.** Per the user, no Brain fixes are being made now. Two were prototyped on scratch branches\n(`eb-exp1`, `eb-exp2`) to measure them, and nothing was committed.\n\n## Where the time goes today\n\n| Process | Time | Dominant step |\n|---|---|---|\n| setup, `cli` (5,621 checkpoints) | **never finishes**, projected 3.9-5.6 h | 5,621 sequential `entire checkpoint explain <id>` calls, 2.5-3.6 s each (99.99% subprocess wait) |\n| setup, entire-graph, cold | 105 s, 1.45 GB RSS | history index 52.4 s (50%), pattern layer 31.6 s (30%), semantic 10.3 s, entity index 7.4 s |\n| setup re-run, nothing changed | 17.3 s | entity index 10.6 s (re-runs 10 `graph diff` every time) |\n| setup re-run after one `remember` | 37 s | pattern corpus rebuilt, 21.5 s |\n| first `brief` after setup | 6.4 s, 609 MB | lazy history search-index build, 5.37 s (sqlite write) |\n| warm `brief` | 610 ms | status 383 ms, graph context 127 ms, history rank 67 ms |\n| first `brain_brief` over MCP | 3.9 s | waits about 3.5 s on a background worker's write lock |\n| `doctor` | 1.94 s, 788 MB RSS | decodes all 158k history records |\n| `status` | 445 ms | |\n| `distill --dry-run` | 6.7 s | CPU over 1.27 GB of transcripts |\n| recall / query / search | 72-128 ms | fine |\n| MCP tool list | 31.8 KB (~8k tokens) per agent session | 32 tool schemas |\n\n## Proposed fixes, ranked by user-visible saving\n\n| # | Fix | Where | Saving | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Stop the per-checkpoint host-CLI call during export.** The list call (5.8 s) already returns session ids, agent and date for all checkpoints. Use those fields, or a batch call, an immutable per-checkpoint cache, bounded parallelism, or the local fast path for local checkpoints. | session export (repos with a separate checkpoint remote, e.g. cli) | **hours: from \"never finishes\" to finishing.** About 4-5.6 h becomes seconds to minutes. | timing log: 336 calls in 1,198 s; 2.5-3.6 s each |\n| 2 | **`historyTerms` normalizes the whole text once per keyword**, up to 26x. Normalize once, then match. | history index | **history 52.4 s → 31.5 s (−40%), setup −17.7 s.** Measured, with identical output (157,977 records, same sha256). | CPU profile: `historyTerms` 18.2 s, of which `normalizeHistorySearchText` 16.3 s |\n| 3 | **Typed JSON decode** instead of untyped maps, and stream the records instead of holding them all in memory. | history index | estimated −10 s CPU and a much smaller heap than the 1.45 GB peak | profile: `json.Unmarshal` 14.2 s; `runtime.madvise` 11.4 s; GC 8.5 s |\n| 4 | **Parse each transcript once** for history, episodes and corpus. Today it is parsed three times. Write the staging DB with sqlite fast-write pragmas and one transaction. | pattern layer + history | **estimated 15-25 s** off a cold setup | profile: second and third parse 11 s + 6.2 s CPU; about 13 s off-CPU in sqlite, one transaction per session |\n| 5 | **Build the history search index during setup**, not lazily on the first brief. | brief | **first brief 6.4 s → about 0.6 s** | profile: `openHistoryFTS` 6.49 s |\n| 6 | **SPLIT (corrected by the review):** brain PR #474 fixes the LEGACY-store variant (export aborted on a divergent cached transcript in a non-checkpoint-backed brain). The loop this study reproduced is a DIFFERENT bug and survives #474 (reviewer built ef41d238; the same 3 jobs still go stale): a **session-ref collision**. conversationSessionRef = sha(repo, branch, sid); an empty branch maps to \"main\" in both jobs and receipts, so a session with an unknown/ copy and a main/ copy shares one ref with two digests. The receipt keeps the first copy, the job keeps the last, and they never match (conversation_session.go:40-52, memory_cmd.go:591-594/656-668, memory_receipts.go:180-187). Fix: make jobs and receipts pick the same copy (fact e60bf39e: do not reject duplicate ids). memory_cmd.go:1162 is correct. The first-MCP-brief lock wait (about 3.9 s) is separate and also survives #474. | memory worker, MCP | first MCP brief 3.9 s → about 0.5 s (lock part); 3/229 sessions never project | review: BRAIN-SPEEDUPS-REVIEW.md |\n| 7 | **Run the 10 `graph diff` calls in parallel or as one batch**, and skip commits already indexed. | entity index | **6-8 s off every warm setup and watch tick** | 10 sequential calls, 7.4-10.6 s |\n| 8 | **Cache the git filter probe and the `graph store` result per process.** brief calls `graph store` twice. | brief, status, small-repo setup | **brief −26% (610 → 449 ms), status −48% (445 → 234 ms).** Measured, identical output. Probe is 19% of small-repo setup. | prototype `eb-exp2` |\n| 9 | **Run the status probes in parallel with graph context** in brief. | brief | estimated 100-250 ms | estimate only |\n| 10 | **doctor reads health metadata** instead of decoding the full index. | doctor | **about −1.5 s and −750 MB** | profile: `loadBrainHistoryIndex` 1.10 s, `decodeBrainHistoryIndex` 0.91 s |\n| 11 | **Pattern corpus: update incrementally** after a `remember`, instead of rebuilding it. | setup re-run | about −20 s on the next re-run (37 s → 17 s) | re-run after remember: corpus rebuilt 21.5 s |\n| 12 | **Trim the MCP tool list** (descriptions, rarely used tools behind one dispatcher). | every agent session | about 8k tokens of context per session | tools/list = 31.8 KB |\n| 13 | **Host dispatch overhead** of about 30-50 ms per `entire graph` call (Brain → host CLI → graph). Call the graph binary directly when it is known. | brief, setup | about 30-50 ms per graph call | shim timings |\n\n## Bugs found while measuring (not speed, but user-visible)\n\n- **cli semantic index fails**: the graph refuses `index --head` on a partial-clone repo, and the real `devenv/cli` has the same configuration.\n- **No-egress session export fails** on the partial clone (missing blobs).\n- **Every new commit silently drops code context from brief** (\"search store not built for this commit\") until the graph store is rebuilt.\n- **The memory-worker retry loop** above (#6) runs forever-ish in the background.\n\n## If these were all done (rough, cold setup on entire-graph)\n\n~~105 s → roughly 45-55 s~~. **Corrected by the review: about 65-80 s.** Only #2 (−17.7 s) was measured; #3 and #4 together\nare −8-15 s, not −25-35 s, because their cost is page-cache spill, not parsing. The first brief is about 0.6 s only if the FTS\nstops being fully rebuilt after every history change (#5 alone relocates the cost). The first MCP brief drops to about 0.5 s\nonly with M1 fixed. cli setup finishes at all once #1 lands (explain only non-local ids; fail fast on a partial clone).\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}},"displayPath":"../devenv-worktrees/brain-timing/BRAIN-SPEEDUPS.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b5d1c33a-018b-4760-bc4a-f3763a3145e7","timestamp":"2026-10-25T07:17:22.175Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/brain-timing/BRAIN-SPEEDUPS.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Brain: proposed speed and efficiency fixes\n2\t\n3\t> **Read BRAIN-SPEEDUPS-REVIEW.md first.** It corrects several estimates (realistic cold setup is about 65-80 s, not 45-55 s), splits #6, refutes parts of #1/#7/#8, and adds M1: brief fails with brain_locked after 10.5 s while any writer holds the lock. **Re-diagnosed by the Brain session:** the reader takes the lock only at response-write time, for microseconds. It is the erasure linearization boundary (`retrieval_transport.go:113`) and must stay. The cause is writers holding the same lock across full rebuilds (`history_fts.go:142/337`, `doc_fts.go:49`, `pattern_workspace.go:72`, `history.go:307`, `history_delta.go:1015`). Fix: build-then-swap on the writers. M1, #11 and the FTS rebuild are one fix.\n4\t\n5\tMeasured 2026-10-24 on entire-brain `main` 318e4b05 (= installed v0.2.0-nightly), with the graph at d4a0c186.\n6\tIsolated store, no daemon. Quiet-machine numbers (load 7-10).\n7\tFull process maps: `PROCESS-MAP.md`. Raw data: `results/`. Profiles: `profiles/`, `results/pprof/`.\n8\t\n9\t**Status: proposals only.** Per the user, no Brain fixes are being made now. Two were prototyped on scratch branches\n10\t(`eb-exp1`, `eb-exp2`) to measure them, and nothing was committed.\n11\t\n12\t## Where the time goes today\n13\t\n14\t| Process | Time | Dominant step |\n15\t|---|---|---|\n16\t| setup, `cli` (5,621 checkpoints) | **never finishes**, projected 3.9-5.6 h | 5,621 sequential `entire checkpoint explain <id>` calls, 2.5-3.6 s each (99.99% subprocess wait) |\n17\t| setup