{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"9585062edc8fdeac976d09d33a88187fbf7b7e9d8d7b75243d239f2a2e0cd95f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I've finished checking the Go 1.26 bug. It's smaller than the Brain session's framing, and the fix is not a simple dependency bump. Recording it now."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011zSVgq7QGYMiU87dwjZGPY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json,fcntl,os,datetime\nlk=open('.claude/todo-board.lock','w'); fcntl.flock(lk,fcntl.LOCK_EX)\np='.claude/todo-board.json'; d=json.load(open(p))\nfor r in d['tasks']:\n    if r.get('id')=='G-followups' and 'MEASURED new(expr)' not in r['title']:\n        r['title']+=(\" | MEASURED new(expr) impact: blast radius today 4 files/26 calls, all entiredb tests (cli, entire-graph, entire-brain, entire-api: 0, go/ast count). Damage is local: all 77 funcs in the 4 files present in symbols; outer CALLS edges present for 10/10 functions whose call line has new(\\\"..\\\"). Only what is INSIDE the new(...) arg is at risk. Fix is NOT a dep bump: upstream tree-sitter-go master (0.25.0+) special_argument_list still requires a type first; needs a patched grammar vendored under internal/sem/grammars/go (11 grammars already vendored there) + upstream PR.\")\nd['updated']=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec='seconds')\njson.dump(d,open(p+'.tmp','w'),indent=2); os.replace(p+'.tmp',p)\np='/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/semantic-layer-handoff-state.md'; s=open(p).read()\na=\"Both in G-followups.\\n\"\ns=s.replace(a,\"Both in G-followups. Measured: 4 files/26 calls (entiredb tests only); defs 77/77 + outer CALLS 10/10 survive (tree-sitter error recovery) — only exprs inside new(...) at risk. Upstream tree-sitter-go master still needs a type in special_argument_list → fix = vendored patched grammar, not a bump.\\n\",1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Record new(expr) impact measurement and fix path"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HuLW5zT3UY57nZaw22m3QB","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"new(expr): measured impact is small, fix is vendored grammar","message":"I measured both of your questions, and the framing does overreach. Please don't tell the user that neighbors/impact are \"confidently wrong\" on Go 1.26. The data:\n- **Blast radius today:** 4 files and 26 calls, all in entiredb test files. cli, entire-graph, entire-brain and entire-api have zero. I counted with a go/ast walk for new() whose argument is a literal, call, binary, unary or composite expression. `new(x)` with a bare identifier is ambiguous with a type and parses fine.\n- **The damage is local.** tree-sitter's error recovery keeps the surrounding structure. All 77 functions in the 4 files are in `graph symbols` (9/9, 40/40, 12/12, 16/16). In github_email_identities_test.go, all 10 functions that call UpsertGitHubEmailIdentity have the CALLS edge in `graph edges`, including the ones whose only call line contains new(\"4242\"). What's at risk is what sits inside the new(...) argument, for example the Add call in new(now.Add(...)). I didn't measure that case.\n- **It's not a dependency bump.** Upstream tree-sitter-go master (0.25.0, Sep 2025) still defines special_argument_list for new/make as '(' $._type, ... ')', so even the latest grammar rejects new(0). The fix is a patched grammar vendored under internal/sem/grammars/go, where graph already vendors 11 grammars, plus an upstream PR.\n\nSo it's a real correctness gap, growing as Go 1.26 code spreads, but small and contained today. I'd call it below the degrade-label confusion in user impact right now. Both are in G-followups with these numbers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
