{"parentUuid":"f2334ca1-ddc6-4243-b839-3700bf8569ce","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTqAfdyeGnEwm2L7XTqd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BYC3N77u8gKbvuxSb8Y6he","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"nested-call residual refuted too; entire-graph hit is Ruby","message":"Both open points are closed, and the residual worry is refuted too. (1) The entire-graph hit is internal/sem/capability_relation_union_test.go:471, `def point_sum(p = Point.new(0, 0))`. That's Ruby inside a Go raw-string fixture, so my go/ast walk correctly excluded it. (2) Nested calls: the corpus couldn't settle it, because TestService_ReadModelBacklog also calls now.Add on 4 other lines, so the edge exists either way. I used a scratch repo under v0.6.0 instead: `func onlyInsideNew() *int { return new(g()) }` plus two controls. graph flags x.go E_PARSE_ERROR, yet the edges stream has CALLS onlyInsideNew->g, control->h and afterBroken->h. So no fact loss has been measured anywhere: symbols, outer calls and nested calls all survive. In practice E_PARSE_ERROR on Go 1.26 new(expr) is a spurious warning, and its real cost is feeding the \"degraded\" label. That folds this ticket into the degrade one. A cheaper fix than a vendored grammar: for Go, check go/parser's verdict before emitting E_PARSE_ERROR (graph already imports go/parser in 3 files). Recorded on G-followups.","type":"message","recipient":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","recipient_kind":"name","content":"Both open points are closed, and the residual wor…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":981,"cache_read_input_tokens":213125,"output_tokens":1462,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":182},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":981,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1462,"cache_read_input_tokens":213125,"cache_creation_input_tokens":981,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":981},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BYC3N77u8gKbvuxSb8Y6he":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"nested-call residual refuted too; entire-graph hit is Ruby","message":"Both open points are closed, and the residual worry is refuted too. (1) The entire-graph hit is internal/sem/capability_relation_union_test.go:471, `def point_sum(p = Point.new(0, 0))`. That's Ruby inside a Go raw-string fixture, so my go/ast walk correctly excluded it. (2) Nested calls: the corpus couldn't settle it, because TestService_ReadModelBacklog also calls now.Add on 4 other lines, so the edge exists either way. I used a scratch repo under v0.6.0 instead: `func onlyInsideNew() *int { return new(g()) }` plus two controls. graph flags x.go E_PARSE_ERROR, yet the edges stream has CALLS onlyInsideNew->g, control->h and afterBroken->h. So no fact loss has been measured anywhere: symbols, outer calls and nested calls all survive. In practice E_PARSE_ERROR on Go 1.26 new(expr) is a spurious warning, and its real cost is feeding the \"degraded\" label. That folds this ticket into the degrade one. A cheaper fix than a vendored grammar: for Go, check go/parser's verdict before emitting E_PARSE_ERROR (graph already imports go/parser in 3 files). Recorded on G-followups."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"7c85b8dc-8450-4a38-a282-26a35ae07503","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgTqAfNrLqhw21xsFTCjy","requestRef":"db7180d8-f31b-470c-801a-23982ab5ff2e","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"3aef4d51-f2b8-42ee-b0f4-5d9b48ac7657","timestamp":"2026-10-28T02