{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"f42e2ee792aa09d2156c6ca395c0ebbed04cd55855fe3a7fd74e08cf16544e9c","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PapieiXbVyEoCpwnahjnq8","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json,fcntl,os,datetime\nlk=open('.claude/todo-board.lock','w'); fcntl.flock(lk,fcntl.LOCK_EX)\np='.claude/todo-board.json'; d=json.load(open(p))\nfor r in d['tasks']:\n    if r.get('id')=='G-followups' and 'SYNTHETIC new(g())' not in r['title']:\n        r['title']+=(\" | SYNTHETIC new(g()) test (scratch repo, v0.6.0): file flagged E_PARSE_ERROR yet CALLS onlyInsideNew->g, control->h, afterBroken->h all present. No measured fact loss anywhere => in practice a SPURIOUS warning that feeds 'degraded'. Cheaper fix option: for Go, defer to go/parser's verdict (graph already imports go/parser in 3 files) before emitting E_PARSE_ERROR; vendored grammar still the full fix.\")\nd['updated']=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec='seconds')\njson.dump(d,open(p+'.tmp','w'),indent=2); os.replace(p+'.tmp',p)\np='/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/semantic-layer-handoff-state.md'; s=open(p).read()\na=\"fix = vendored patched grammar, not a bump.\\n\"\ns=s.replace(a,\"fix = vendored patched grammar, not a bump. Synthetic `new(g())` test: E_PARSE_ERROR flagged but CALLS ->g still emitted → no measured loss; effectively a spurious warning feeding 'degraded' (cheap fix: defer to go/parser for Go).\\n\",1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Record synthetic nested-call result"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BYC3N77u8gKbvuxSb8Y6he","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"fix-brain-initialization-ux","summary":"nested-call residual refuted too; entire-graph hit is Ruby","message":"Both open points are closed, and the residual worry is refuted too. (1) The entire-graph hit is internal/sem/capability_relation_union_test.go:471, `def point_sum(p = Point.new(0, 0))`. That's Ruby inside a Go raw-string fixture, so my go/ast walk correctly excluded it. (2) Nested calls: the corpus couldn't settle it, because TestService_ReadModelBacklog also calls now.Add on 4 other lines, so the edge exists either way. I used a scratch repo under v0.6.0 instead: `func onlyInsideNew() *int { return new(g()) }` plus two controls. graph flags x.go E_PARSE_ERROR, yet the edges stream has CALLS onlyInsideNew->g, control->h and afterBroken->h. So no fact loss has been measured anywhere: symbols, outer calls and nested calls all survive. In practice E_PARSE_ERROR on Go 1.26 new(expr) is a spurious warning, and its real cost is feeding the \"degraded\" label. That folds this ticket into the degrade one. A cheaper fix than a vendored grammar: for Go, check go/parser's verdict before emitting E_PARSE_ERROR (graph already imports go/parser in 3 files). Recorded on G-followups."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
