xt":"list all open prs bh extenrla and let me know if their trahs/not worth it or acutally good"}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5","hash":"284f12f4b89f870796d50be9de8fc3db7ecac895383d8b65d9f6a8c92979c6d8","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Called the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/go-overlay-cannot-mutate-data-files.md\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t---\n2\tname: go-overlay-cannot-mutate-data-files\n3\tdescription: \"go test -overlay swaps BUILD inputs only, so mutating testdata/vendored files through it silently proves nothing — the test reads the real file at runtime\"\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: feedback\n7\t  originSessionId: b0108f24-43a4-413e-94c9-bd361b6fafc5\n8\t  modified: 2026-11-04T23:41:36.651Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t`go test -overlay=ov.json` replaces files **the compiler reads**. It does not\n12\tintercept `os.ReadFile`, `os.ReadDir` or `filepath.Glob` at runtime. So a\n13\tmutation aimed at a data file — testdata, a vendored `parser.c`, a JSON\n14\tmanifest — is simply ignored, the test reads the real bytes from disk, and the\n15\trun comes back green.\n16\t\n17\tMeasured 2026-11-04 on entire-graph #357. I wrote two new tests (a digest over\n18\t62 vendored grammar files, and a manifest-vs-disk check), then \"proved\" they\n19\tcould fail by overlaying a mutated `swift/scanner.c` and a mutated\n20\t`grammars.json`. Both mutations came back **ok**. I nearly recorded them as\n21\tmutation-proven. Re-run against the real files — mutate in place, then\n22\t`git checkout -- <path>` — and all three mutations went red immediately.\n23\t\n24\t**Why:** a green run under a mutation reads exactly like a correctly-passing\n25\ttest, which is the failure mode [[green-suite-is-not-evidence]] and\n26\t[[mutation-didnt-fire-suspect-the-test]] are about. Overlay is the right tool\n27\tfor mutating **Go source** (it kept the worktree clean across four tool-code\n28\tmutations in the same session), so the method is sound for half the job and\n29\tsilently useless for the other half.\n30\t\n31\t**How to apply:** mutate Go code with `-overlay`; mutate data files in place\n32\tand restore with `git checkout -- <path>` (verify with `git status --porcelain`\n33\tafter). If a mutation that should break a test does not, suspect the mutation\n34\treached nothing before suspecting the test — check whether the thing you\n35\tchanged is read at build time or at run time. Related:\n36\t[[verification-harness-false-pass]], [[verified-by-weakening-doctrine]].\n37\t\n\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-357-review/internal/sem/grammars/swift/VALIDATION.md\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Swift 0.7.4 compatibility validation\n2\t\n3\tCompared on macOS arm64 against main at\n4\t`28382d8ee2f0cb4ebc962255a3d8cbd364d2f258`, using the ten Swift repositories\n5\tand commits in `bench/repos.lock.json` (2026-11-02).\n6\t\n7\t## Per-file parser audit\n8\t\n9\tParsed all 24,946 tracked, regular `.swift` files within the provider's 4 MiB\n10\tlimit with `TreeSitterParser.ParseWithStatus`, on both revisions. Counts below\n11\tare Swift-only parser results, not whole-repository graph totals. The Swift\n12\tcompiler corpus includes deliberately malformed inputs.\n13\t\n14\t| Repository | Parse errors before | Parse errors after | Symbols before | Symbols after |\n15\t| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n16\t| Alamofire/Alamofire | 10 | 4 | 7,388 | 7,388 |\n17\t| ReactiveX/RxSwift | 45 | 45 | 13,734 | 13,734 |\n18\t| SnapKit/SnapKit | 0 | 0 | 511 | 511 |\n19\t| SwiftyJSON/SwiftyJSON | 0 | 0 | 693 | 693 |\n20\t| airbnb/lottie-ios | 2 | 1 | 4,758 | 4,758 |\n21\t| apple/swift | 10,581 | 9,736 | 180,725 | 181,979 |\n22\t| onevcat/Kingfisher | 4 | 3 | 4,504 | 4,504 |\n23\t| pointfreeco/swift-composable-architecture | 201 | 51 | 9,861 | 9,872 |\n24\t| realm/realm-swift | 30 | 21 | 11,039 | 11,093 |\n25\t| vapor/vapor | 53 | 7 | 4,232 | 4,232 |\n26\t\n27\tNet change: 1,058 fewer parse errors and 1,319 additional symbols. No previously\n28\tclean file in the nine application/library repositories gains a parse error.\n29\tThe 20 newly flagged compiler fixtures contain invalid syntax (including\n30\tunterminated comments, malformed conditions and compiler-crasher inputs).\n31\tThey must remain diagnostics rather than being hidden by source masking.\n32\t\n33\t## Targeted preservation checks\n34\t\n35\t- Hummingbird 2.28.0's `HTTP2ServerConnectionManager+StateMachine.swift` retains\n36\t  exactly the baseline's 42 symbol IDs and 113 relation triples, with no parse\n37\t  failure. The unpatched PR produced only 23 symbols and 59 relations.\n38\t  **Not re-measured on the final parser**, for the same reason as the\n39\t  whole-repository totals below: these counts predate the last scanner and\n40\t  grammar fixes. The per-file audit above is the current measurement.\n41\t- Regression tests cover conditional attributes and extensions, ownership\n42\t  switches, conditional switch cases and nested case bodies, multiline\n43\t  associated-type constraints, compiler diagnostics, internal/versioned\n44\t  compilation conditions, inline `#endif`, comment lookahead (including leading\n45\t  operators and closures after comments), string `canImport` versions, flags\n46\t  named like predicates, and macros whose names begin with a directive word.\n47\t- Graph assertions preserve declaration ranges, attributes, containment,\n48\t  calls and field reads/writes. Malformed-source tests require parse failures.\n49\t- All 296 upstream grammar corpus tests and the upstream highlighting/outline\n50\t  tests pass. Applying `compatibility.patch` to the pinned upstream commit and\n51\t  generating with tree-sitter-cli 0.24.7 reproduces `parser.c` and `scanner.c`\n52\t  byte for byte.\n53\t\n54\tThe full Go suite, targeted race tests, vet and license checks passed on the\n55\tfinal source tree. Reproduce them with:\n56\t\n57\t```sh\n58\tgo test -timeout 20m ./...\n59\tgo test -race ./internal/sem -run 'Swift|IdentityRevision' -count=1\n60\tgo vet ./...\n61\tbash scripts/gen-notices.sh --check\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\tThe default ten-minute run timed out in the CLI and semantic packages while the\n65\tcorpus benchmark ran concurrently, without an assertion failure. The final\n66\tsuite uses the larger timeout above and runs without that benchmark load.\n67\t\n68\t## Whole-repository graph comparison\n69\t\n70\tMeasured before the final scanner and grammar fixes, which changed three\n71\t`apple/swift` parse results and five symbols in the per-file audit above. The\n72\ttotals below have not been re-measured on the final parser.\n73\t\n74\tEvery repository retains or increases its total symbols and relations:\n75\t\n76\t| Metric | Main | Patched grammar | Change |\n77\t| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n78\t| Symbols | 411,903 | 413,217 | +1,314 |\n79\t| Relations | 1,342,900 | 1,343,247 | +347 |\n80\t| Parse failures | 12,437 | 11,382 | -1,055 |\n81\t\n82\tApple gains 89 relations, Composable Architecture 37, Realm 157 and Vapor 64;\n83\tthe other six repositories retain their relation totals. These totals measure\n84\tcoverage, not identity equivalence: the targeted Hummingbird comparison above\n85\tchecks the actual symbol IDs and relation triples.\n86\t\n87\tFor this comparison, build `./cmd/graph-bench` at each revision\n88\tand run it against the same lock-pinned corpus:\n89\t\n90\t```sh\n91\tgraph-bench -languages Swift -cache /path/to/corpus -out /path/to/results -skip-clone\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\tThat benchmark includes other languages found in each repository. Its totals\n95\ttherefore differ from the Swift-only table above. Concurrent local validation\n96\tis not a controlled performance experiment; timing is not a speedup claim.\n97\t\n\nNote: /Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-357-review/internal/sem/swift_grammar_regression_test.go was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-357-review/internal/sem/vendored_grammars_test.go\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\tpackage sem\n2\t\n3\timport (\n4\t\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n5\t\t\"encoding/hex\"\n6\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n7\t\t\"os\"\n8\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n9\t\t\"regexp\"\n10\t\t\"sort\"\n11\t\t\"strconv\"\n12\t\t\"strings\"\n13\t\t\"testing\"\n14\t)\n15\t\n16\t// vendoredGrammarsDigest pins the bytes of every vendored grammar under\n17\t// internal/sem/grammars. Changing a grammar changes parse results, and the\n18\t// snapshot caches are keyed on the git tree of the *indexed repository*, which\n19\t// a parser change does not touch — so IdentityRevision is the only thing that\n20\t// stops records built by the old grammar being served for unchanged source.\n21\t//\n22\t// Nothing else in the suite can notice a grammar change: there is no Swift\n23\t// fixture, and the one golden holding Swift symbols is byte-identical across\n24\t// the 0.5.0 -> 0.7.4 swap apart from its identity_revision field. Two earlier\n25\t// tests tried to cover this by comparing IdentityRevision against the previous\n26\t// value, which cannot fail while TestParserIdentityRevisionsSnapshotAndCaches\n27\t// pins it exactly; this compares the grammar bytes instead.\n28\t//\n29\t// When this fails because a grammar was deliberately refreshed: decide whether\n30\t// parse results change (they almost always do), bump IdentityRevision in\n31\t// provider.go if so, and then update the constant below to the printed digest.\n32\tconst vendoredGrammarsDigest = \"769dfdcf34ccef15c993e9989535761389ff967e7708e2f0e72cf19a4f932bf4\"\n33\t\n34\tfunc TestVendoredGrammarsMatchTheManifest(t *testing.T) {\n35\t\tgrammars := loadGrammarManifest(t)\n36\t\tif len(grammars) == 0 {\n37\t\t\tt.Fatal(\"grammars.json lists no grammars\")\n38\t\t}\n39\t\tlanguageVersion := regexp.MustCompile(`(?m)^#define LANGUAGE_VERSION (\\d+)\\s*$`)\n40\t\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n41\t\tfor _, g := range grammars {\n42\t\t\tif seen[g.Language] {\n43\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s is listed twice in grammars.json\", g.Language)\n44\t\t\t}\n45\t\t\tseen[g.Language] = true\n46\t\t\tdir := filepath.Join(\"grammars\", g.Language)\n47\t\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(dir); err != nil {\n48\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"grammars.json lists %s, but %s does not exist: %v\", g.Language, dir, err)\n49\t\t\t\tcontinue\n50\t\t\t}\n51\t\t\t// The manifest's files list is what a refresh treats as the managed set:\n52\t\t\t// a file on disk it omits would be deleted, and one it names that is\n53\t\t\t// absent would never be replaced.\n54\t\t\tonDisk := managedGrammarFiles(t, dir)\n55\t\t\trecorded := append([]string(nil), g.Files...)\n56\t\t\tsort.Strings(recorded)\n57\t\t\tif strings.Join(onDisk, \",\") != strings.Join(recorded, \",\") {\n58\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: grammars.json files = %v, on disk = %v\", g.Language, recorded, onDisk)\n59\t\t\t}\n60\t\t\tparser, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(dir, \"parser.c\"))\n61\t\t\tif err != nil {\n62\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: %v\", g.Language, err)\n63\t\t\t\tcontinue\n64\t\t\t}\n65\t\t\tm := languageVersion.FindSubmatch(parser)\n66\t\t\tif m == nil {\n67\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: parser.c has no #define LANGUAGE_VERSION\", g.Language)\n68\t\t\t\tcontinue\n69\t\t\t}\n70\t\t\tif got := string(m[1]); got != strconv.Itoa(g.ABI) {\n71\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: parser.c is ABI %s, grammars.json records %d\", g.Language, got, g.ABI)\n72\t\t\t}\n73\t\t}\n74\t\t// Every grammar directory must be described, or a refresh of it has no\n75\t\t// origin to fetch and nothing records that it carries local changes.\n76\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(\"grammars\")\n77\t\tif err != nil {\n78\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n79\t\t}\n80\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n81\t\t\tif e.IsDir() && !seen[e.Name()] {\n82\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"grammars/%s is vendored but absent from grammars.json\", e.Name())\n83\t\t\t}\n84\t\t}\n85\t}\n86\t\n87\tfunc TestVendoredGrammarBytesAreTiedToTheIdentityRevision(t *testing.T) {\n88\t\tsum := sha256.New()\n89\t\tvar files []string\n90\t\tfor _, g := range loadGrammarManifest(t) {\n91\t\t\tdir := filepath.Join(\"grammars\", g.Language)\n92\t\t\tfor _, rel := range managedGrammarFiles(t, dir) {\n93\t\t\t\tfiles = append(files, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Join(dir, rel)))\n94\t\t\t}\n95\t\t}\n96\t\tsort.Strings(files)\n97\t\tfor _, f := range files {\n98\t\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(f)\n99\t\t\tif err != nil {\n100\t\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n101\t\t\t}\n102\t\t\t// Hash the path too, so moving a grammar is a change.\n103\t\t\tsum.Write([]byte(f))\n104\t\t\tsum.Write([]byte{0})\n105\t\t\tsum.Write(data)\n106\t\t}\n107\t\tgot := hex.EncodeToString(sum.Sum(nil))\n108\t\tif got != vendoredGrammarsDigest {\n109\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"vendored grammar bytes changed (%d files).\\n\"+\n110\t\t\t\t\"  digest now: %s\\n\"+\n111\t\t\t\t\"  pinned:     %s\\n\"+\n112\t\t\t\t\"If a grammar was refreshed, decide whether parse results change; if they do, \"+\n113\t\t\t\t\"bump IdentityRevision in internal/sem/provider.go (currently %q) so cached \"+\n114\t\t\t\t\"records built by the old grammar are not served, then set \"+\n115\t\t\t\t\"vendoredGrammarsDigest to the digest above.\",\n116\t\t\t\tlen(files), got, vendoredGrammarsDigest, IdentityRevision)\n117\t\t}\n118\t}\n119\t\n120\ttype grammarManifestEntry struct {\n121\t\tLanguage string   `json:\"language\"`\n122\t\tFiles    []string `json:\"files\"`\n123\t\tABI      int      `json:\"abi\"`\n124\t}\n125\t\n126\tfunc loadGrammarManifest(t *testing.T) []grammarManifestEntry {\n127\t\tt.Helper()\n128\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"grammars\", \"grammars.json\"))\n129\t\tif err != nil {\n130\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n131\t\t}\n132\t\tvar out []grammarManifestEntry\n133\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &out); err != nil {\n134\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n135\t\t}\n136\t\treturn out\n137\t}\n138\t\n139\t// managedGrammarFiles lists the files in a vendored grammar directory that a\n140\t// refresh owns, using the same rule as cmd/grammar-refresh: parser.c,\n141\t// scanner.c, headers, tree_sitter/*.h and the licence. binding.go and the\n142\t// Swift notes are deliberately excluded.\n143\tfunc managedGrammarFiles(t *testing.T, dir string) []string {\n144\t\tt.Helper()\n145\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(dir)\n146\t\tif err != nil {\n147\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n148\t\t}\n149\t\tvar out []string\n150\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n151\t\t\tname := e.Name()\n152\t\t\tupper := strings.ToUpper(name)\n153\t\t\tswitch {\n154\t\t\tcase e.IsDir() && name == \"tree_sitter\":\n155\t\t\t\theaders, err := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, \"tree_sitter\", \"*.h\"))\n156\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n157\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n158\t\t\t\t}\n159\t\t\t\tfor _, h := range headers {\n160\t\t\t\t\tout = append(out, \"tree_sitter/\"+filepath.Base(h))\n161\t\t\t\t}\n162\t\t\tcase e.IsDir():\n163\t\t\tcase name == \"parser.c\" || name == \"scanner.c\",\n164\t\t\t\tstrings.HasSuffix(name, \".h\"),\n165\t\t\t\tstrings.HasPrefix(upper, \"LICENSE\"),\n166\t\t\t\tstrings.HasPrefix(upper, \"COPYING\"):\n167\t\t\t\tout = append(out, name)\n168\t\t\t}\n169\t\t}\n170\t\tsort.Strings(out)\n171\t\treturn out\n172\t}\n173\t\n\nNote: /Users/suhaan/devenv-worktrees/eg-357-review/cmd/grammar-refresh/refresh_test.go was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nA plan file exists from plan mode at: /Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/lets-plan-cool-then-fluffy-walrus.md\n\nPlan contents:\n\n# Plan: make entire-graph solid on main, and actually useful to agents, end to end\n\n## Context\n\nThe fixes and the integration both fall short today. These figures were measured this week.\n\n### The fixes are not on main\n- About 47 PRs are open with green CI. None is merged.\n- Every one is blocked only by the Entire Gates approvals sub-gate, which shows 0 approvals.\n- All 47 are authored by the user, so GitHub will not let the user approve them.\n- The tip PR #317 contains 37 of them: 219 commits, about 10.8k lines of production code.\n- 4 high-severity agent-review findings are open, and the full diff has never been reviewed end to end. The #315 review timed out.\n\n### Agents barely use the graph\nIn the user's real sessions (1,640 transcripts), only about 38 genuine graph locate calls were found. Several causes stack up:\n- **Wrong metric.** 77% of the 76K \"exploration\" calls are not code locating at all (`| head` pipes, logs, JSON), so the stats metric is misleading.\n- **Exact names.** 70.7% of code searches are exact-name lookups, where grep is the right tool: sub-second, versus a graph median of 14.7s.\n- **Subagents are never told.** 0 of 1,545 subagent prompts mention the graph, and subagents do 88% of code searches.\n- **Cold and slow.** The guide's command omits `--head`, so every call rebuilds the index: 10–30s in fresh worktrees, where 75% of searches happen.\n- **Unreliable.** 18% of genuine graph calls failed (stale binary, degraded output, timeout), and answers were re-checked with grep 20 of 31 times.\n\n### What the pilot showed\n- Forcing the agent to call the graph as a tool costs a whole turn, about 20K tokens: +26% tokens with no accuracy gain.\n- Injecting the graph answer into the prompt instead (no tool call) was token-neutral, +0.7%.\n- It resolved more tasks in both stages that tested it, 25 vs 23 and 26 vs 23, but neither difference is significant.\n- The graph finds the right file in 66/93 tasks, but the exact lines in only 11/93.\n\n### Intended outcome\n- The fixes are on main and released, and agents get a fast, correct graph.\n- The graph reaches agents **without costing them a turn**: main sessions and subagents both.\n- Usage and value are measured honestly.\n\n### Dropped, because the evidence says they are low value\n- **Graph-first rate as a KPI.**\n- **Competing with grep on exact names.**\n- **Line-level targeting as a token lever.** Edit requires a prior Read anyway.\n- **PostToolUse impact notes on every edit.**\n- **Per-worktree prewarming.** Cache keys are tree-hash and per-user, so a clean tree can reuse HEAD.\n- **A significance-powered accuracy A/B.** About 180 pairs, unaffordable.\n\n## Order (user decision, 2026-10-05)\n\n\"First meet our goal\": make the graph fast, reach agents, and prove its value **before** dealing with approvals and\nmerging. New sequence:\n\n| Order | Phase | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Phase 0 (gate) | first |\n| 2 | Phase 1 steps 1–2 (fix highs, layered review) | parallel with 0 |\n| 3 | Phase 2 and Phase 3 | built as PRs **stacked on #317**, so nothing waits for main |\n| 4 | Phase 4 | after 3 |\n| 5 | Phase 5, including the paid non-inferiority check (approved, about 60 pairs, stays under the $50 cap) | after 4 |\n| 6 | Phase 1 steps 3–5 (merge, release) | last |\n\nThe goal is met when, in the user's own sessions:\n- the graph answer reaches the main session and its subagents without costing a turn;\n- answer files are used at least 60% of the time;\n- failures are under 3%;\n- the paid check shows no token or accuracy penalty.\n\nFor local dogfooding before the release, a binary built from the stack tip is installed under a separate path, never\nover the user's `entire`.\n\n## Owners\n- **Claude:** agentsetup guide text, stats, the plugin and hooks, analyses, reviews.\n- **Codex:** the Go `sem` and search lanes. Ownership of Go files is agreed over the bridge before Phase 2.\n- **User:** approves and merges every PR. Nothing is merged without explicit approval.\n\n## Phase 0: free offline gate (Claude, parallel with Phase 1)\nReplay real prompts through `entire graph query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 2048` at recoverable commits:\n- the 1,545 subagent prompts;\n- the first prompt of each main session.\n\nMeasure the hit rate: answer files the agent later Read or Edited.\n- **Gate:** a subagent hit rate under 40% means do not build subagent injection (Phase 3b).\n- Reuse `ART/runtime/locbench/coverage.py` and the transcript parsers from `analysis-L/`.\n\n## Phase 1: land it\n1. **Close the 4 high findings on the stack tip**, each with a regression test that is shown to fail without the fix:\n   - #313: add a CI leg built with `GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2`.\n   - #312: depth bound plus fuzz test on `observeStripPrefixValue` (`internal/sem/go_route_binding.go:787`).\n   - #309: unit test for the signature-budget double charge.\n   - #277: `[complete]` re-read wording, read alongside the T206 medium.\n2. **Review by layer.** Codex reviews the `sem` and search layers of #315 and #317; Claude reviews the rest; then each cross-checks the other. Run agent findings per layer, not on 219 commits at once. Deliver the user a ranked risk list.\n3. **Merge with merge commits, never squash, each step user-approved:**\n   - #290;\n   - retarget #294 to main;\n   - retarget #297 to main;\n   - retarget #317 to main (covers #315 and #316).\n   - Then #270, then #264, regenerating goldens after each because the two conflict. Then #320 (Phase 3a fixes first). Then #253 and #146 after a rebase.\n4. **Close superseded PRs** only after `git cherry` shows their patches are on main. #296 and #142 are close-or-rescope decisions.\n5. **Tag a nightly and refresh the installed `entire`.** It is currently a 2026-09-18 build that predates the directive guide and errors on newer flags.\n\n**Success:**\n- main CI green on Linux, macOS and Windows;\n- 0 open highs;\n- 0 \"unknown command\" or flag errors over one day of dogfooding.\n\n## Phase 2: fast, correct default path (Codex: Go; Claude: guide)\n1. **Auto-HEAD.** When the working tree's tracked files are clean (`git diff-index --quiet HEAD`), use the HEAD cache automatically; when dirty, keep working-tree mode.\n   - Relevant code: `internal/cli/env.go` (`resolveCacheDir`) and `internal/sem/provider.go` (`repoKey`).\n   - Fallback if this slips: put `--head` in the guide command.\n2. **Guide** (`internal/agentsetup/guide.go`):\n   - Command becomes `query --head --format agent --max-context-bytes 4096`. 4 KiB is the measured study-5 setting.\n   - Add: \"grep for an exact identifier, graph for concepts and relationships.\"\n   - Keep the directive tone; drop the 88-line strict ritual from defaults.\n3. **Profile the 4s cached query** after #313 lands (streaming cache).\n\n**Success:**\n- fresh worktree at an indexed commit: p50 ≤ 2s (now 10–30s);\n- genuine-call failure rate < 3% (now 18%);\n- default payload ≤ 4 KB (now 12 KB).\n\n## Phase 3: zero-turn integration (Claude, plugin, opt-in first)\n### a. Fix #320\n- Use `--head` or auto-HEAD, an 8s timeout, and a version and capabilities probe once per session.\n- Fix the Windows failure, probably the `python3` dependency.\n- Resolve the 2 medium findings.\n- Later, port the hook to an `entire graph hook prompt` Go subcommand, so the hook ships with a binary that understands its flags.\n\n### b. Subagent coverage (only if Phase 0 passes)\n- A plugin `PreToolUse` hook on the `Agent` tool builds a graph answer from `tool_input.prompt`. It delivers the answer via `SubagentStart` `additionalContext`, which is preferred over `updatedInput` to avoid touching permission flows. Verify the mechanism first with a 10-minute live test.\n- Run one query at a time per tree hash, and skip files the parent session already received.\n- Default-on for the user's own sessions; opt-in for everyone else.\n\n**Success:**\n- hook fires on ≥ 90% of eligible prompts;\n- answer-file hit rate ≥ 60%;\n- added p95 latency ≤ 5s;\n- grep calls per subagent down ≥ 20%.\n\n## Phase 4: answer quality (Codex)\n- File recall from 66/93 to ≥ 75/93 on held-out repos, with no study-5 regression.\n- Confirm #302's exact-name path answers in ≤ 1s.\n\n## Phase 5: measure honestly (Claude)\n1. **Hook telemetry.** Hooks append local JSONL (fired, latency, bytes, files). `entire graph stats` joins it with the agent's later Reads and Edits.\n2. **Exploration metric.** Count only code-locate calls, building on #278 and #304 once they land.\n3. **Paid check (user-approved, at the end).** One paired run of the hook bundle on fresh tasks, sized to stay under\n   the $50 cap with a provable worst case.\n   - Spend to date: $21.80.\n   - Worst-case cell: $4, from the 50-turn limit.\n   - So the stage threshold is about $20 (21.80 + 20 + 2 × 4 ≤ 50), which admits about $12 of observed spend: roughly\n     60 pairs.\n   - Scored as non-inferiority: token CI upper bound ≤ +10%, and resolved b ≤ c.\n   - Uses the existing pilot8 harness, Stage-A-style (fresh, gold- and P4-eligible tasks), and freezes the hook bundle\n     exactly as shipped.\n4. **Default-on decision** rests on that telemetry plus the non-inferiority result, not on statistical significance.\n\n## Dependencies\n- Phases 0 and 1 run in parallel.\n- Phase 2's Go work is stacked on the #317 branch, so it does not wait for #317 to land on main.\n- The #320 fix (3a) needs nothing.\n- 3b needs Phase 0.\n- Phase 5 needs the release from Phase 1 step 5.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Nobody reviews 10.8k lines end to end | Layered Codex and Claude cross-review now; the human approver is decided at the merge step, which comes last. The user authored every PR, so a teammate (or an explicit user override) is needed then. Release #290 alone first. |\n| Auto-HEAD answers from stale source | Dirty-tree detection falls back to the working tree. |\n| Parallel subagents spike CPU | Serialize queries per tree hash. |\n| Hooks delay prompts | 8s timeout; fail open; telemetry on latency. |\n\n## Verification\n- **Phase 1:**\n  - the mutation-proven regression tests above;\n  - main CI green on all three OSes;\n  - after release, `entire graph query --head --format agent` smoke-tested in a fresh worktree and in this devenv;\n  - `entire graph capabilities --json` reports the new flags.\n- **Phase 2:**\n  - time 10 queries in a fresh worktree, cold and warm;\n  - the agentsetup golden tests for guide text.\n- **Phase 3:**\n  - the hermetic hook tests (#320 already has 10, every guard mutation-proven);\n  - a live Claude Code session showing the injected context on the first prompt and in a spawned subagent;\n  - telemetry JSONL populated.\n- **Phase 5:** `entire graph stats` on one week of the user's real sessions, compared against this week's baseline: about 38 genuine locates, an 18% failure rate, and 0 of 1,545 subagents reached.\n\n\nIf this plan is relevant to the current work and not already complete, continue working on it.\n\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: loop\nPath: bundled:loop\n\n# /loop — schedule a recurring or self-paced prompt\n\nParse the input below into `[interval] <prompt…>` and schedule it.\n\n## Parsing (in priority order)\n\n1. **Leading token**: if the first whitespace-delimited token matches `^\\d+[smhd]$` (e.g. `5m`, `2h`), that's the interval; the rest is the prompt.\n2. **Trailing \"every\" clause**: otherwise, if the input ends with `every <N><unit>` or `every <N> <unit-word>` (e.g. `every 20m`, `every 5 minutes`, `every 2 hours`), extract that as the interval and strip it from the prompt. Only match when what follows \"every\" is a time expression — `check every PR` has no interval.\n3. **No interval**: otherwise, the entire input is the prompt and you'll self-pace dynamically (see \"Dynamic mode\" below).\n\nIf the resulting prompt is empty, show usage `/loop [interval] <prompt>` and stop.\n\nExamples:\n- `5m /babysit-prs` → interval `5m`, prompt `/babysit-prs` (rule 1)\n- `check the deploy every 20m` → interval `20m`, prompt `check the deploy` (rule 2)\n- `run tests every 5 minutes` → interval `5m`, prompt `run tests` (rule 2)\n- `check the deploy` → no interval → dynamic mode, prompt `check the deploy` (rule 3)\n- `check every PR` → no interval → dynamic mode, prompt `check every PR` (rule 3 — \"every\" not followed by time)\n- `5m` → empty prompt → show usage\n\n## Offer cloud first\n\nBefore any scheduling step, check whether EITHER is true:\n- the parsed interval (rule 1 or 2) is **≥60 minutes**, or\n- regardless of which rule matched, the original input uses daily phrasing (\"every morning\", \"daily\", \"every day\", \"each night\", \"every weekday\")\n\nIf either is true, call AskUserQuestion first:\n- `question`: \"This loop stops when you close this session. Set it up as a cloud schedule instead so it keeps running?\"\n- `header`: \"Schedule\"\n- `options`: `[{label: \"Cloud schedule (recommended)\", description: \"Runs in Anthropic's cloud even after you close this session\"}, {label: \"This session only\", description: \"Runs in this terminal until you exit\"}]`\n\nIf they pick **Cloud schedule**: do NOT call CronCreate. Invoke the `schedule` skill directly via the Skill tool with `args` set to their original input verbatim (e.g. `Skill({skill: \"schedule\", args: \"every morning tell me a joke\"})`), then follow that skill's instructions to completion. Do NOT tell the user to run /schedule themselves. **Then stop — do not continue to any section below** (no CronCreate, no ScheduleWakeup, no \"execute the prompt now\").\nIf they pick **This session only**:\n- If the trigger was a parsed ≥60-minute interval (rule 1 or 2): continue below with that interval.\n- If the trigger was daily phrasing only (rule 3, no parsed interval): do NOT call CronCreate. Explain that a daily-cadence loop won't fire before this session closes, so there's nothing useful to schedule locally — suggest they either pick Cloud schedule, or re-run `/loop` with an explicit shorter interval (e.g. `/loop 1h <prompt>`) if they want a session loop. Then stop.\nIf neither trigger condition was met: continue below.\n\n## Fixed-interval mode (rules 1 and 2)\n\nConvert the interval to a cron expression:\n\n| Interval pattern      | Cron expression     | Notes                                    |\n|-----------------------|---------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| `Nm` where N ≤ 59   | `*/N * * * *`     | every N minutes                          |\n| `Nm` where N ≥ 60   | `0 */H * * *`     | round to hours (H = N/60, must divide 24)|\n| `Nh` where N ≤ 23   | `0 */N * * *`     | every N hours                            |\n| `Nd`                | `0 0 */N * *`     | every N days at midnight local           |\n| `Ns`                | treat as `ceil(N/60)m` | cron minimum granularity is 1 minute  |\n\n**If the interval doesn't cleanly divide its unit** (e.g. `7m` → `*/7 * * * *` gives uneven gaps at :56→:00; `90m` → 1.5h which cron can't express), pick the nearest clean interval and tell the user what you rounded to before scheduling.\n\nThen:\n1. Call CronCreate with: `cron` (the expression above), `prompt` (the parsed prompt verbatim), `recurring: true`.\n2. Briefly confirm: what's scheduled, the cron expression, the human-readable cadence, that recurring tasks auto-expire after 7 days, and that the user can cancel sooner with CronDelete (include the job ID). Only if you did NOT show the cloud-offer AskUserQuestion above (i.e., neither trigger condition applied), end the confirmation with this exact line on its own, italicized: `_Runs until you close this session · For durable cloud-based loops, use /schedule_`. If the user already answered that question, omit this line.\n3. **Then immediately execute the parsed prompt now** — don't wait for the first cron fire. If it's a slash command, invoke it via the Skill tool; otherwise act on it directly.\n\n## Dynamic mode (rule 3 — no interval)\n\nThe user wants you to self-pace. Decide what makes the next iteration worth running — a passage of time, or an observable event.\n\n1. **Run the parsed prompt now.** If it's a slash command, invoke it via the Skill tool; otherwise act on it directly.\n2. **If the next run is gated on an event** (CI finishing, a log line matching, a file changing, a PR comment) and no Monitor is already running for it: arm one now with `timeout_ms: 1800000`. Its events arrive as `<task-notification>` messages and wake this loop immediately — you do not wait for the ScheduleWakeup deadline. A monitor expires after at most 30 minutes and tells you; on later iterations call TaskList first and re-arm only if no monitor for it is still running.\n3. **Decide whether the loop continues.** If the task needs another iteration, call ScheduleWakeup with:\n   - `delaySeconds`: with a Monitor armed this is the **fallback heartbeat** — how long to wait if no event fires (lean 1200–1800s; idle ticks more frequent than the task needs are pure overhead). Without a Monitor this is the cadence — pick based on what you observed. Read the tool's own description for cache-aware delay guidance.\n   - `reason`: one short sentence on why you picked that delay.\n   - `prompt`: the full original /loop input verbatim, prefixed with `/loop ` so the next firing re-enters this skill and continues the loop. For example, if the user typed `/loop check the deploy`, pass `/loop check the deploy` as the prompt.\n   - `noop`: `true` if this tick changed nothing (\"still waiting\", \"quiet hold\"); `false` if it did something worth keeping. Consecutive `noop: true` ticks collapse in the terminal.\n   If it doesn't need another iteration, stop instead (step 6) — re-arming is a per-turn choice, not a default.\n4. **After the wakeup is armed, briefly confirm**: that you're self-pacing, whether a Monitor is the primary wake signal, that you ran the task now, and what fallback delay you picked. This must be ordinary visible response text — the user cannot see your thinking/reasoning, so an update written only there is invisible to them. Make it the last thing in the turn, then end the turn.\n5. **If you were woken by a `<task-notification>`** rather than this prompt: handle the event in the context of the loop task, then make the same decision. If the loop should continue, call ScheduleWakeup again with the same `prompt` and the same 1200–1800s `delaySeconds` from the schedule step above (the Monitor remains the wake signal; the new wakeup is only the fallback heartbeat), then write the same brief update as visible text. If the event means the work is finished, stop (step 6).\n6. **To stop the loop** — the task is complete, further iterations can't make progress, or the user asked you to stop — call ScheduleWakeup with `stop: true` (no other fields) and TaskStop any Monitor you armed (use TaskList to find the task ID if it is no longer in context). Then write the loop's outcome for the user as ordinary visible response text — a stopped loop has no next tick to surface it. Stopping is the loop's normal ending — the user can restart it anytime with /loop. Before you stop, send a one-line outcome via PushNotification — the user may be away and waiting to hear it's done. Skip this if you're stopping because the user just told you to; they're already here.\n\n## Input\n\ndrive entire-graph PR #357 to merge-ready: land my verified fixes on it, close the remaining findings, keep CI green. NEVER merge it, and never merge anything else.\n\n---\n\n### Skill: response\nPath: userSettings:response\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/suhaan/.claude/skills/response\n\n# Chat-style response\n\nWrite the way a real engineer types in Slack or Discord. Short, direct, scannable. Not an essay.\n\n## Rules\n- No em-dashes. None. Do not use the long dash to join clauses. Use a period, a comma, parentheses, or split the line.\n- No long flowing sentences. Short lines, one idea per line.\n- Bullets over paragraphs. Lead with the answer, then supporting points as bullets.\n- Human and casual. Plain words, contractions are fine. No corporate or essay voice.\n- No fluff. Skip \"I hope this helps\", \"Let me know if\", \"Great question\", preambles, and windups.\n- Light formatting only: bold the one key thing, bullets for lists, backticks for commands and identifiers.\n\n## Shape\n- First line is the answer or the status.\n- Then a few short bullets with the details (aim for 2 to 5, not a wall).\n- If an action is needed, put it last on its own line.\n\n## Feel\n\nNot this:\n\n> I went ahead and looked into the issue, and it turns out the readiness check was pointing at an endpoint that no longer exists, which is the reason it kept timing out and shutting itself down.\n\nThis:\n\n> found it. the readiness check hits `:9000/status`, which is gone.\n> - backend is actually healthy (`:8180/healthz` returns ok)\n> - so it timed out and killed a working server\n>\n> fix: point the check at `/healthz`.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Slack progress report on reasoning capture: what I found (M2 was never shipped, brain review returns 0 findings because sessions/ is empty), what I decided to do about each, what's built and where it stands\n\nThe following tools just became available and are ready to use:\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__batch\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__guide\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__update\n\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nArtifactComments\nArtifactData\nCronCreate\nCronDelete\nCronList\nDesignSync\nEndConversation\nEnterPlanMode\nEnterWorktree\nExitPlanMode\nExitWorktree\nFetchInboxMessage\nLSP\nListMcpResourcesTool\nMonitor\nNotebookEdit\nPushNotification\nReadMcpResourceDirTool\nReadMcpResourceTool\nRemoteTrigger\nSendMessage\nSendUserFile\nTaskStop\nWebSearch\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__browser_batch\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__computer\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__file_upload\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__find\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__form_input\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__get_page_text\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__gif_creator\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__javascript_tool\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__list_connected_browsers\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__navigate\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__read_console_messages\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__read_network_requests\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__read_page\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__resize_window\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__select_browser\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__shortcuts_execute\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__shortcuts_list\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__switch_browser\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_close_mcp\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_context_mcp\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_create_mcp\nmcp__claude-in-chrome__upload_image\nmcp__claude_ai_Canva__authenticate\nmcp__claude_ai_Canva__complete_authentication\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__create\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__delete\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__export\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__query\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__read\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__copy_file\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__create_file\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__download_file_content\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_metadata\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_permissions\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__list_recent_files\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__read_file_content\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__search_files\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__share_file\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__trash_file\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__update_file\nmcp__claude_ai_Linear